1
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:26,611
Hello , I 'm Christophe Gans ,

2
00:00:26,777 --> 00:00:31,699
the director of the film you 're going to watch , Silent Hill .

3
00:00:31,866 --> 00:00:34,035
This film

4
00:00:34,201 --> 00:00:35,745
is very important to me

5
00:00:35,912 --> 00:00:39,498
inasmuch as it 's related to another passion of mine ,

6
00:00:39,665 --> 00:00:43,002
besides cinema , which is video games .

7
00:00:43,628 --> 00:00:46,047
I 've made two other feature-length films ,

8
00:00:46,213 --> 00:00:48,341
Crying Freeman and Brotherhood of the Wolf ,

9
00:00:48,507 --> 00:00:51,594
which were films about my passion for cinema ,

10
00:00:52,219 --> 00:00:53,387
Asian cinema

11
00:00:53,554 --> 00:00:58,476
and European genre cinema of the 1960s .

12
00:00:58,726 --> 00:00:59,852
And , of course ,

13
00:01:00,144 --> 00:01:04,649
after a while I wanted to tackle something a bit more personal ,

14
00:01:04,815 --> 00:01:06,901
something less referential ,

15
00:01:07,068 --> 00:01:09,528
and maybe to talk about another

16
00:01:11,238 --> 00:01:12,531
of my hobbies ,

17
00:01:12,990 --> 00:01:14,951
namely video games ,

18
00:01:15,117 --> 00:01:18,788
a domain which , for the moment , is largely uncharted ,

19
00:01:18,955 --> 00:01:22,959
as regards its analysis , as regards its history ...

20
00:01:23,209 --> 00:01:26,253
And maybe this let me make , in Silent Hill ,

21
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:28,965
not a more personal film ,

22
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:32,802
but at least a more individual one , than my previous two .

23
00:01:33,594 --> 00:01:34,428
Of course ,

24
00:01:35,137 --> 00:01:39,892
adapting a video game isn 't like adapting a book ,

25
00:01:40,059 --> 00:01:41,727
or remaking another film .

26
00:01:41,894 --> 00:01:45,481
It 's more , for me , like a travel diary .

27
00:01:45,648 --> 00:01:48,567
What I mean is , Silent Hill is a game

28
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:51,612
in which you can lose yourself .

29
00:01:51,779 --> 00:01:54,740
It takes you on a trip in another world ,

30
00:01:54,907 --> 00:01:58,411
another country , which is , of course , virtual ,

31
00:01:58,577 --> 00:02:00,871
but still very real for the player ,

32
00:02:01,038 --> 00:02:05,001
who spends hours and hours wandering around in it .

33
00:02:05,626 --> 00:02:08,212
And ... and so ,

34
00:02:08,379 --> 00:02:11,257
adapting a video game is quite a special exercise .

35
00:02:11,424 --> 00:02:12,717
I didn 't want the film

36
00:02:12,883 --> 00:02:15,428
to simply have the title Silent Hill .

37
00:02:15,594 --> 00:02:18,097
I didn 't want the film to just be ...

38
00:02:18,264 --> 00:02:22,727
I didn 't want it to simply use the video game .

39
00:02:22,893 --> 00:02:28,441
But I didn 't want it to be just a cinematographic transcription .

40
00:02:28,607 --> 00:02:31,527
I wanted it to be a film about my experience as a player ,

41
00:02:31,694 --> 00:02:34,238
a film which , in a way , communicates

42
00:02:34,405 --> 00:02:37,158
what I feel when I 'm playing .

43
00:02:37,324 --> 00:02:41,454
So it was a bit of a gamble because , in a way ,

44
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:44,874
it ran counter to certain narrative rules of cinema ,

45
00:02:45,041 --> 00:02:46,834
which are well established ,

46
00:02:47,001 --> 00:02:50,546
and which I consider to be very conservative .

47
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:54,508
I talked about it to two other directors , who are also gamers ,

48
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:57,344
Nicolas Boukhrief and Roger Avary .

49
00:02:57,511 --> 00:03:01,807
And we quickly agreed to try an adaptation of Silent Hill ,

50
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:03,684
the three of us ,

51
00:03:03,851 --> 00:03:06,687
even though Nicolas Boukhrief 's name

52
00:03:06,854 --> 00:03:09,607
only appears at the end of the credits .

53
00:03:09,774 --> 00:03:12,860
In fact , Nicolas Boukhrief was there at the start

54
00:03:13,027 --> 00:03:16,739
of the adaptation of the video game , along with me .

55
00:03:16,906 --> 00:03:20,576
Our job , at first , consisted

56
00:03:20,743 --> 00:03:23,871
of working out how to transfer not the game ,

57
00:03:24,038 --> 00:03:26,582
but our sensations when playing it .

58
00:03:27,625 --> 00:03:30,086
Now of course , Silent Hill

59
00:03:30,252 --> 00:03:31,962
is a town .

60
00:03:32,129 --> 00:03:34,548
It 's a place .

61
00:03:34,715 --> 00:03:38,344
And it remains more a place than a story .

62
00:03:38,511 --> 00:03:41,430
For me it 's sort of The Twilight Zone ,

63
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:44,725
the equivalent of Rod Serling 's famous TV series ,

64
00:03:44,892 --> 00:03:47,269
a place where anything could happen .

65
00:03:47,436 --> 00:03:49,063
If you were to go there ,

66
00:03:49,230 --> 00:03:53,025
you 'd be confronted with your subconscious , your psyche ,

67
00:03:53,192 --> 00:03:56,445
your fears , obviously , but also perhaps with your hopes .

68
00:03:56,612 --> 00:03:58,614
It 's a universe

69
00:04:00,032 --> 00:04:01,909
that 's deeply mystical ,

70
00:04:02,076 --> 00:04:04,578
so the people who come to Silent Hill

71
00:04:04,745 --> 00:04:08,207
can have different types of experiences .

72
00:04:08,374 --> 00:04:11,252
Quite clearly , in the film ,

73
00:04:11,418 --> 00:04:14,463
the place Silent Hill is a world

74
00:04:14,630 --> 00:04:16,757
where several dimensions meet .

75
00:04:16,924 --> 00:04:20,344
One of these , the fog dimension , is , in a way ...

76
00:04:20,511 --> 00:04:23,305
an expiatory dimension .

77
00:04:23,472 --> 00:04:28,269
It 's sort of the dimension of ... purgatory .

78
00:04:28,435 --> 00:04:32,773
There 's also the rust dimension , which is the dimension of punishment .

79
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:33,941
Hell .

80
00:04:34,108 --> 00:04:38,112
And at one point in the film I thought it 'd be interesting to show

81
00:04:38,279 --> 00:04:40,948
the paradise dimension .

82
00:04:41,115 --> 00:04:44,076
And this scene under the tree is the scene

83
00:04:44,243 --> 00:04:46,537
which gives us , quite clearly ,

84
00:04:46,704 --> 00:04:50,833
the main character 's own personal paradise .

85
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,128
A simple scene , under a tree , in a field ,

86
00:04:54,295 --> 00:04:56,839
with the daughter she loves more than anything .

87
00:04:59,592 --> 00:05:01,135
As you can see ,

88
00:05:01,302 --> 00:05:04,138
the father , played by Sean Bean ,

89
00:05:04,305 --> 00:05:07,766
has quickly been pushed to one side in the narrative .

90
00:05:08,142 --> 00:05:09,518
That was my intention .

91
00:05:09,685 --> 00:05:12,605
I wanted the character played by Sean Bean

92
00:05:12,771 --> 00:05:15,524
to appear at the beginning and the end ,

93
00:05:15,691 --> 00:05:18,986
and for the film proper to have only female characters .

94
00:05:19,153 --> 00:05:23,991
And then the studio which pre-bought the film , TriStar ,

95
00:05:24,158 --> 00:05:27,369
asked for more of the male character .

96
00:05:27,536 --> 00:05:30,331
They were very good to me ,

97
00:05:30,497 --> 00:05:33,125
very flexible and indulgent .

98
00:05:33,292 --> 00:05:38,547
Their only request was for the male character to appear more .

99
00:05:38,714 --> 00:05:41,717
They were worried the film would become too feminine ,

100
00:05:41,884 --> 00:05:44,220
and that some of its basic audience ,

101
00:05:44,386 --> 00:05:47,848
who are obviously teenagers and young men ,

102
00:05:48,015 --> 00:05:51,977
because the whole world of video games

103
00:05:52,144 --> 00:05:54,021
is still very male ...

104
00:05:54,188 --> 00:05:56,232
Less so , but it 's still the case .

105
00:05:56,398 --> 00:05:58,817
So they asked me for a man .

106
00:05:58,984 --> 00:06:03,697
So we carefully slipped in Sean Bean 's character

107
00:06:03,864 --> 00:06:05,282
throughout the film .

108
00:06:05,449 --> 00:06:08,786
This caused problems , which I 'll come back to ,

109
00:06:08,953 --> 00:06:11,830
but it was their only request .

110
00:06:13,123 --> 00:06:16,794
I tried to make sure that the atmosphere and rhythm of the film

111
00:06:16,961 --> 00:06:20,047
took their cue from the music .

112
00:06:20,214 --> 00:06:21,799
The music for the film ,

113
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:25,803
which was rearranged rather subtly by Jeff Danna ,

114
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:28,889
is in fact the work of Akira Yamaoka ,

115
00:06:29,056 --> 00:06:32,768
who has been the producer of the series Silent Hill ,

116
00:06:33,227 --> 00:06:34,895
since Silent Hill 2 .

117
00:06:35,187 --> 00:06:39,066
He 's the embodiment , if you like ,

118
00:06:39,233 --> 00:06:42,820
of orthodox Silent Hill-ness , if such a thing exists .

119
00:06:42,987 --> 00:06:46,782
And Akira Yamaoka ... It was very important to me that the film music

120
00:06:46,949 --> 00:06:50,744
should be music written by Akira Yamaoka .

121
00:06:51,078 --> 00:06:52,580
When you play the game ,

122
00:06:52,746 --> 00:06:56,083
you are immersed , often for hours at a time ,

123
00:06:56,709 --> 00:06:57,960
in this music ,

124
00:06:58,127 --> 00:07:01,880
so I couldn 't imagine any other sort of music for Silent Hill .

125
00:07:02,256 --> 00:07:06,719
But this music also has its own , very individual internal rhythm ,

126
00:07:07,761 --> 00:07:12,141
so I worked at synching the film to the music 's range ,

127
00:07:12,308 --> 00:07:14,893
getting this floating feel .

128
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:18,188
The scene we 've just seen , with Radha Mitchell

129
00:07:18,355 --> 00:07:23,110
driving down that road at the end of the day ,

130
00:07:23,277 --> 00:07:25,863
with the girl drawing in the back seat ,

131
00:07:26,030 --> 00:07:27,489
is , for me , a scene

132
00:07:27,656 --> 00:07:32,077
which lets Akira Yamaoka 's music take over the film

133
00:07:32,244 --> 00:07:34,496
and take over its rhythm .

134
00:07:40,294 --> 00:07:45,132
The girl 's drawings , particularly the one of a sort of symbol ,

135
00:07:45,758 --> 00:07:49,428
a cross on the top of a building , which we guess is religious ...

136
00:07:49,595 --> 00:07:53,557
This is obviously the first clue , within the film ,

137
00:07:53,724 --> 00:07:56,143
of this treasure hunt

138
00:07:56,310 --> 00:07:59,021
which the heroine will have to follow

139
00:07:59,188 --> 00:08:01,065
to get her daughter back .

140
00:08:01,231 --> 00:08:02,566
On this level ,

141
00:08:02,733 --> 00:08:05,986
the film slips into the mode of a video game ,

142
00:08:06,153 --> 00:08:10,032
meaning that all through the film we 'll get these clues ,

143
00:08:10,199 --> 00:08:13,494
some more subtle than others ,

144
00:08:13,786 --> 00:08:15,621
some more coded than others ,

145
00:08:16,246 --> 00:08:19,416
which will help her find her way

146
00:08:19,583 --> 00:08:23,045
through the weird labyrinth that is Silent Hill .

147
00:08:23,504 --> 00:08:28,008
It 's the moment when the film becomes more

148
00:08:28,175 --> 00:08:31,220
the transcription of a game

149
00:08:31,387 --> 00:08:34,223
than a story told in a traditional way .

150
00:08:40,688 --> 00:08:43,107
I mentioned Rod Serling 's The Twilight Zone .

151
00:08:43,273 --> 00:08:47,569
I think that another obvious influence , at least in this first part ,

152
00:08:49,696 --> 00:08:53,075
is the series Alfred Hitchcock Presents ,

153
00:08:53,283 --> 00:08:54,451
which I love .

154
00:08:54,618 --> 00:08:58,080
This consisted , like the series The Twilight Zone ,

155
00:08:58,247 --> 00:09:00,499
of tales with a twist ,

156
00:09:00,666 --> 00:09:03,419
usually stories of wives

157
00:09:04,712 --> 00:09:07,214
running away from home , of isolated people

158
00:09:09,258 --> 00:09:11,427
in little towns ,

159
00:09:11,969 --> 00:09:15,722
bumpkins confronted with certain problems ,

160
00:09:15,889 --> 00:09:18,600
being chased by a policeman and so on .

161
00:09:18,767 --> 00:09:20,686
Typical Hitchcock stories .

162
00:09:20,853 --> 00:09:23,313
That was on my mind when I shot this scene ,

163
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:28,193
on the one hand because of Radha Mitchell 's " Grace Kelly " aspect ,

164
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,654
which really attracted me to her ,

165
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:33,574
and , on the other , by her action

166
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:36,785
of taking her daughter without telling her husband ,

167
00:09:36,952 --> 00:09:40,080
whereby she ends up in this state of feeling guilty

168
00:09:40,247 --> 00:09:42,291
that will cause her to make mistakes .

169
00:09:42,458 --> 00:09:45,669
Also , when I shot this scene by the side of the road ,

170
00:09:45,836 --> 00:09:48,589
in this little diner ,

171
00:09:48,755 --> 00:09:51,925
I gave a lot of thought to the way

172
00:09:52,092 --> 00:09:55,512
those series in the 1950s were filmed ,

173
00:09:55,679 --> 00:09:58,599
with very simple , pure directing styles ,

174
00:09:58,765 --> 00:10:01,018
with just ...

175
00:10:01,226 --> 00:10:04,229
with very straightforward shots .

176
00:10:05,397 --> 00:10:08,650
And this allowed me to get as quickly as possible

177
00:10:08,817 --> 00:10:12,488
to the town of Silent Hill , with a narrative economy

178
00:10:12,654 --> 00:10:16,825
which was exactly what I liked about that TV series .

179
00:10:31,715 --> 00:10:33,801
This shot here

180
00:10:33,967 --> 00:10:38,388
is a sort of " plane 's-eye view " of the countryside .

181
00:10:39,181 --> 00:10:42,643
It represents , to me , the " map " in a video game .

182
00:10:43,769 --> 00:10:47,773
For example , when you 're playing a role-playing game ,

183
00:10:47,940 --> 00:10:51,568
there 's always a moment when your character 's in a map ,

184
00:10:51,735 --> 00:10:55,656
when you see him moving through a scaled-down landscape ,

185
00:10:55,822 --> 00:10:57,908
and you can get your bearings .

186
00:10:58,075 --> 00:11:00,828
You see that there 's nothing around you ,

187
00:11:00,994 --> 00:11:03,956
with maybe a town or evidence of civilisation

188
00:11:04,122 --> 00:11:06,333
at the other end of the map ,

189
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:09,086
which you have to get to .

190
00:11:09,253 --> 00:11:11,088
Hence that shot .

191
00:11:11,255 --> 00:11:14,049
I also liked the fact that usually in American films

192
00:11:14,216 --> 00:11:17,845
you 're always right at road level when a car 's moving .

193
00:11:18,011 --> 00:11:21,390
The energy of the car always has to be very evident .

194
00:11:21,557 --> 00:11:23,850
But I tried to distance myself ,

195
00:11:24,017 --> 00:11:27,854
in an attempt to go on respecting that dreamlike aspect ,

196
00:11:28,146 --> 00:11:31,108
that floating feeling , and to create the idea

197
00:11:31,275 --> 00:11:34,403
that the car is tiny from the huge viewpoint we have ,

198
00:11:34,570 --> 00:11:38,699
and that there 's no one , apparently , in the area she 's heading towards .

199
00:11:38,866 --> 00:11:41,618
It 's dark and there 's no one in that landscape .

200
00:11:41,785 --> 00:11:45,330
And the idea of solitude is clearly introduced for the first time

201
00:11:45,497 --> 00:11:48,875
by that shot in the film .

202
00:11:50,127 --> 00:11:54,673
Still trying to respect the aesthetics of those TV series

203
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:56,091
of the 1950s ,

204
00:11:56,258 --> 00:12:00,137
I faked all of the car 's interior shots .

205
00:12:00,304 --> 00:12:04,474
Practically all those shots were filmed the same day ,

206
00:12:04,725 --> 00:12:08,687
using the so-called " poor man 's process " ,

207
00:12:08,854 --> 00:12:10,856
where you shake the car .

208
00:12:11,023 --> 00:12:13,900
I used a green screen ,

209
00:12:14,067 --> 00:12:16,445
and then added all the backgrounds .

210
00:12:16,653 --> 00:12:20,115
To inject a bit of realism into these shots ,

211
00:12:20,407 --> 00:12:23,994
we digitally added dust on the windscreen ,

212
00:12:24,161 --> 00:12:25,787
and mud splatters ,

213
00:12:26,371 --> 00:12:28,165
creating a dirty look ,

214
00:12:28,332 --> 00:12:32,336
which makes the special effects work very well , I think .

215
00:12:34,171 --> 00:12:36,423
So it took the film 13 minutes

216
00:12:36,798 --> 00:12:38,926
to get to Silent Hill .

217
00:12:39,092 --> 00:12:42,846
From this moment on , the film is quite a faithful transcription

218
00:12:43,013 --> 00:12:45,140
of the first game , which begins

219
00:12:45,474 --> 00:12:48,435
with this accident that 's caused

220
00:12:48,602 --> 00:12:51,855
by this mysterious apparition in the middle of the road .

221
00:12:52,314 --> 00:12:55,317
If you 've watched the trailer carefully ,

222
00:12:55,484 --> 00:12:56,693
you 'll have noticed

223
00:12:56,860 --> 00:12:59,488
that you see the car literally pass through

224
00:12:59,655 --> 00:13:01,948
the apparition in the road .

225
00:13:02,366 --> 00:13:04,159
It was ghostlike .

226
00:13:04,326 --> 00:13:06,954
I wanted to do away with that idea .

227
00:13:07,245 --> 00:13:10,749
The trailer was finished before the film was fully edited .

228
00:13:10,916 --> 00:13:13,877
But I found that idea a bit heavy-handed

229
00:13:14,044 --> 00:13:15,712
and a bit too easy ,

230
00:13:15,879 --> 00:13:18,298
like , " Oh , it was a ghost ! "

231
00:13:18,465 --> 00:13:21,885
I wanted to be a bit more subtle than that .

232
00:13:22,052 --> 00:13:26,181
On top of that , none of the shots I got from special effects

233
00:13:26,348 --> 00:13:28,308
were very convincing ,

234
00:13:28,475 --> 00:13:32,437
so I did away with that . And the accident works well as it is .

235
00:13:33,230 --> 00:13:37,359
From this moment on , practically all the film was shot in the studio .

236
00:13:37,651 --> 00:13:39,987
The real challenge of the film

237
00:13:40,153 --> 00:13:43,699
was to have all its means geared to the art direction ,

238
00:13:43,865 --> 00:13:47,077
to create a totally artificial universe ,

239
00:13:47,244 --> 00:13:50,455
but which is nevertheless coherent

240
00:13:50,622 --> 00:13:53,625
and of a completely unprecedented scale ,

241
00:13:54,209 --> 00:13:56,253
working only in the studio .

242
00:13:57,087 --> 00:13:59,256
Creating a virtual world ,

243
00:13:59,423 --> 00:14:04,636
using processes that are , at times , very old-fashioned ,

244
00:14:04,803 --> 00:14:07,806
and sometimes very modern , like digital imaging .

245
00:14:07,973 --> 00:14:12,644
So that was the big , big challenge of the film .

246
00:14:13,729 --> 00:14:15,313
In the game ...

247
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:20,610
It 's in the first game that we see these flakes falling through the fog .

248
00:14:20,777 --> 00:14:23,905
Lots of fans have wondered if it 's snow .

249
00:14:24,072 --> 00:14:27,534
But I 've always preferred to think of it as ash .

250
00:14:27,701 --> 00:14:31,204
We 're in this dimension which could be that of purgatory ,

251
00:14:31,371 --> 00:14:32,831
next to hell .

252
00:14:32,998 --> 00:14:36,043
So the fires of hell are producing these ashes

253
00:14:36,209 --> 00:14:39,379
which , cooled down , fall on the fog dimension .

254
00:14:39,546 --> 00:14:43,049
It 's also explained by the history of the town ,

255
00:14:43,216 --> 00:14:45,635
this mining town ,

256
00:14:45,802 --> 00:14:49,181
whose underground mines are still on fire ,

257
00:14:49,347 --> 00:14:53,810
which would explain the ash falling all over the town .

258
00:14:57,105 --> 00:14:59,149
The shot of Radha entering the town ...

259
00:14:59,316 --> 00:15:02,402
The version we 're seeing here , the video version ,

260
00:15:02,569 --> 00:15:05,739
is more finished than what you 'd have seen in the cinema .

261
00:15:05,906 --> 00:15:10,327
I finished the film a week before its American release .

262
00:15:10,494 --> 00:15:11,495
A week before ,

263
00:15:11,661 --> 00:15:15,499
we were still adding shots , special effects , to the film .

264
00:15:15,665 --> 00:15:18,543
There were 800 VFX shots in the film .

265
00:15:18,710 --> 00:15:21,338
We were doing all this at the last moment ,

266
00:15:21,505 --> 00:15:25,133
and we couldn 't finished some shots , like that one there ,

267
00:15:25,300 --> 00:15:29,012
which is improved in the video version .

268
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:32,057
The set of Silent Hill

269
00:15:32,224 --> 00:15:35,894
is , in fact , a practically deserted neighbourhood

270
00:15:36,061 --> 00:15:38,855
of a little town near Toronto .

271
00:15:39,022 --> 00:15:41,983
When I showed photos of the game

272
00:15:42,150 --> 00:15:44,277
to Carol Spier , the production designer ,

273
00:15:44,444 --> 00:15:47,489
she said , " I know this place , "

274
00:15:47,656 --> 00:15:50,492
and she took me to this deserted neighbourhood .

275
00:15:50,659 --> 00:15:53,662
It was handy because hardly anyone lived there ,

276
00:15:53,829 --> 00:15:57,958
and the whole place seemed to have stopped dead in 1965 .

277
00:15:58,125 --> 00:16:02,129
All the buildings , the shops and so on ...

278
00:16:02,295 --> 00:16:05,215
The whole place smelled of abandonment .

279
00:16:05,590 --> 00:16:08,552
A major reason for filming in Toronto

280
00:16:08,718 --> 00:16:12,681
is the fact of having found this amazing film set ,

281
00:16:12,848 --> 00:16:17,060
which was our basis for the entry into Silent Hill .

282
00:16:18,145 --> 00:16:21,648
These shots of her looking for her daughter in the streets ,

283
00:16:21,815 --> 00:16:23,483
and then in this alley ,

284
00:16:23,650 --> 00:16:25,986
are taken straight from the game .

285
00:16:26,695 --> 00:16:28,822
It was important to me to transcribe

286
00:16:28,989 --> 00:16:31,449
what I had felt in Silent Hill ,

287
00:16:31,825 --> 00:16:34,911
a virtual place but which I knew intimately .

288
00:16:35,078 --> 00:16:40,250
I knew every nook and cranny , having spent long hours there .

289
00:16:40,417 --> 00:16:43,420
So I was obsessed with going back there in my film ,

290
00:16:43,587 --> 00:16:46,464
and pinpointing all those places .

291
00:16:46,631 --> 00:16:49,467
It was a sort of obsession with me .

292
00:16:49,634 --> 00:16:51,803
One of the game 's strengths

293
00:16:51,970 --> 00:16:54,055
is the famous siren ,

294
00:16:54,222 --> 00:16:57,767
which announces the passing from one dimension to another .

295
00:16:57,934 --> 00:17:01,605
The siren , to me , represents the success of the game ,

296
00:17:01,771 --> 00:17:06,776
a game which , for many people , appeals to irrational fears ,

297
00:17:06,943 --> 00:17:09,237
or to those of our collective memory ,

298
00:17:09,404 --> 00:17:11,573
especially that siren which , of course ,

299
00:17:11,740 --> 00:17:15,660
is a reference to air raids during the Second World War .

300
00:17:16,369 --> 00:17:20,081
Maybe also to the sirens of concentration camps .

301
00:17:20,248 --> 00:17:23,919
It 's a siren which suddenly makes the world

302
00:17:24,085 --> 00:17:26,713
totally stress-full and dangerous ,

303
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:29,507
without your knowing exactly why .

304
00:17:29,674 --> 00:17:32,344
This is one of the particulars of Silent Hill :

305
00:17:32,510 --> 00:17:36,139
the horror in it is like a bad dream .

306
00:17:36,306 --> 00:17:40,227
It 's not the sort of horror you usually get in films ,

307
00:17:40,393 --> 00:17:42,479
making the audience jump

308
00:17:42,646 --> 00:17:46,483
with the sudden appearances of people or cats in the frame ,

309
00:17:46,650 --> 00:17:49,527
or the slamming of doors .

310
00:17:49,694 --> 00:17:52,489
The fear in Silent Hill is much closer to ...

311
00:17:52,656 --> 00:17:56,576
And this makes it much more fun , I think ...

312
00:17:56,743 --> 00:17:59,204
It 's a fear that envelops you .

313
00:17:59,371 --> 00:18:04,584
It 's something which chills our bones , without our knowing exactly why .

314
00:18:04,751 --> 00:18:08,046
It 's an extremely complex approach to the fantastic ,

315
00:18:08,213 --> 00:18:11,925
inasmuch as some people will be affected by this approach

316
00:18:12,092 --> 00:18:14,511
and others will be left unmoved .

317
00:18:14,678 --> 00:18:19,724
But for me this is the basis of the creation of Silent Hill .

318
00:18:19,891 --> 00:18:23,061
It 's a game which , as well as being inspired by films ,

319
00:18:23,228 --> 00:18:25,855
was also inspired by ...

320
00:18:26,564 --> 00:18:29,985
by a passion of its creators ,

321
00:18:30,151 --> 00:18:33,446
Akira Yamaoka , in particular , for modern art .

322
00:18:34,364 --> 00:18:39,703
And there is , in Silent Hill , the game ,

323
00:18:39,953 --> 00:18:42,080
a contemplative perspective ,

324
00:18:42,247 --> 00:18:47,293
like watching from the sidelines , an atmosphere ,

325
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:52,007
whereby the point of view is slightly skewed ,

326
00:18:52,173 --> 00:18:54,551
to create a sense of unease ,

327
00:18:54,718 --> 00:18:58,388
and to suggest , in an almost insidious way ,

328
00:18:58,555 --> 00:19:00,557
to things which affect us ,

329
00:19:00,724 --> 00:19:05,061
like , of course , the idea of war or genocide ,

330
00:19:05,228 --> 00:19:07,480
but never explicitly .

331
00:19:07,647 --> 00:19:11,609
It 's something very particular , in a way ,

332
00:19:12,652 --> 00:19:15,613
and this will make sure , I think , that the film

333
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:18,908
won 't become associated with other modern horror trends ,

334
00:19:19,075 --> 00:19:23,246
which are all rather formatted . That 's why I 'm grateful

335
00:19:23,413 --> 00:19:27,792
to the people of TriStar for letting me go in this direction ,

336
00:19:27,959 --> 00:19:31,212
which I 'd call a more daring and complex direction

337
00:19:32,672 --> 00:19:34,174
than the current one ,

338
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:37,719
which consists of going overboard with the gore ,

339
00:19:37,886 --> 00:19:43,099
or of going overboard with the sudden , ghost-train surprises .

340
00:19:44,017 --> 00:19:45,727
So this siren ,

341
00:19:45,894 --> 00:19:49,814
for me , is the symbol of this Silent Hill-style fear .

342
00:19:55,236 --> 00:20:00,450
The monsters in Silent Hill , apart from one sort , the cockroaches ...

343
00:20:00,617 --> 00:20:03,787
But apart from the cockroaches ,

344
00:20:03,953 --> 00:20:08,416
all the monsters were created the old-fashioned way .

345
00:20:08,583 --> 00:20:11,586
I mean they were played by actors ,

346
00:20:12,003 --> 00:20:14,589
mainly dancers wearing costumes .

347
00:20:14,756 --> 00:20:17,842
Afterwards , I did a bit of digital make-up

348
00:20:18,009 --> 00:20:20,720
on the monsters ' bodies .

349
00:20:20,887 --> 00:20:24,933
The strange thing was that , after the trailer had come out ,

350
00:20:25,100 --> 00:20:28,228
people said , " The monsters are digital . "

351
00:20:29,187 --> 00:20:32,857
That surprised me because I really didn 't want

352
00:20:33,441 --> 00:20:34,776
people to say that .

353
00:20:34,943 --> 00:20:37,445
Digital technology is absolutely fantastic ,

354
00:20:37,612 --> 00:20:38,696
and I love it .

355
00:20:38,863 --> 00:20:43,118
At the same time , getting dancers to play the monsters ,

356
00:20:43,284 --> 00:20:44,494
and using

357
00:20:44,661 --> 00:20:46,454
their physical prowess

358
00:20:46,621 --> 00:20:48,748
to create weird movements and so on ,

359
00:20:49,541 --> 00:20:50,917
and making these monsters

360
00:20:51,501 --> 00:20:56,548
by sculpting their costumes , as Patrick Tatopoulos did in this film ,

361
00:20:57,298 --> 00:20:59,217
seems to me just as amazing .

362
00:20:59,384 --> 00:21:03,388
It goes back to much more ancient traditions ,

363
00:21:03,555 --> 00:21:06,599
it terms of forms of celebration

364
00:21:06,766 --> 00:21:09,477
and theatricality .

365
00:21:09,811 --> 00:21:12,564
Here , in fact , the Grey Children ...

366
00:21:12,730 --> 00:21:15,150
That 's their name in the game .

367
00:21:15,316 --> 00:21:17,152
The Grey Children are played

368
00:21:17,318 --> 00:21:20,363
by a tiny woman dancer from the Far East ,

369
00:21:20,822 --> 00:21:24,200
who specialises in buto ,

370
00:21:24,868 --> 00:21:28,454
a very special Japanese choreographic art .

371
00:21:29,247 --> 00:21:32,667
All those little creatures were played by her .

372
00:21:33,459 --> 00:21:37,630
She was filmed over and over and the shots were assembled

373
00:21:38,006 --> 00:21:39,048
in post-production .

374
00:21:39,382 --> 00:21:44,304
But she played all the little monsters trying to grab Radha Mitchell .

375
00:21:45,847 --> 00:21:48,683
Deep down , Silent Hill 's structure

376
00:21:48,850 --> 00:21:51,019
is that of a fairy tale ,

377
00:21:51,186 --> 00:21:53,688
with this idea of the character

378
00:21:53,855 --> 00:21:56,900
getting lost in the forest and going to the witch 's house .

379
00:21:57,066 --> 00:21:59,819
Yes , there 's been lots of psychoanalysis

380
00:21:59,986 --> 00:22:02,155
of fairy tales ,

381
00:22:02,322 --> 00:22:05,408
and the fact that they contain a mise en abyme

382
00:22:05,575 --> 00:22:08,495
of our anxieties , our fears ...

383
00:22:08,661 --> 00:22:10,038
It 's clear

384
00:22:10,205 --> 00:22:13,541
that this is important for understanding Silent Hill .

385
00:22:13,708 --> 00:22:17,045
It 's the pleasure of getting lost in the maze ,

386
00:22:17,212 --> 00:22:21,341
of suddenly finding yourself without reference points .

387
00:22:21,591 --> 00:22:25,970
It 's a feeling that I love and I seek it out .

388
00:22:26,137 --> 00:22:29,057
Not just in films , but generally speaking ,

389
00:22:29,224 --> 00:22:31,226
in all sorts of entertainment .

390
00:22:31,392 --> 00:22:36,397
Obviously it 's something which requires , from the audience ,

391
00:22:36,564 --> 00:22:39,776
a certain willing acceptance .

392
00:22:39,984 --> 00:22:43,112
There are lots of people , even with horror films ,

393
00:22:43,279 --> 00:22:46,950
who don 't want the films to go off the rails ,

394
00:22:47,116 --> 00:22:50,078
who want them ruled by rational laws ,

395
00:22:50,245 --> 00:22:52,622
with explanations for everything and so on .

396
00:22:52,789 --> 00:22:56,501
My own tastes draw me to the opposite of that .

397
00:22:56,668 --> 00:22:59,087
You have to drown in the atmosphere .

398
00:22:59,254 --> 00:23:03,216
And I think that the allusions that there are in the game

399
00:23:03,383 --> 00:23:04,676
to modern art

400
00:23:04,842 --> 00:23:07,887
show how you can let yourself go like this .

401
00:23:08,054 --> 00:23:11,891
Modern art is often put down

402
00:23:12,058 --> 00:23:15,853
by people who aren 't willing to let themselves go .

403
00:23:16,229 --> 00:23:20,066
It 's art which requires a certain willing abandon .

404
00:23:20,233 --> 00:23:22,318
And to accept Silent Hill ,

405
00:23:22,735 --> 00:23:24,654
my film and the game ,

406
00:23:24,821 --> 00:23:29,117
you have to enjoy abandoning yourself to an atmosphere ,

407
00:23:29,284 --> 00:23:32,704
you have to enjoy losing your footing ...

408
00:23:32,996 --> 00:23:36,874
So the idea of the film was to constantly show this character

409
00:23:37,041 --> 00:23:41,838
in a state of instability between several worlds .

410
00:23:42,005 --> 00:23:45,049
She 's attacked by something in a sort of night-time ,

411
00:23:45,216 --> 00:23:47,385
which fell very suddenly ,

412
00:23:47,552 --> 00:23:49,804
then she wakes up , in the same place ,

413
00:23:49,971 --> 00:23:51,931
this time in the fog , and so on .

414
00:23:52,098 --> 00:23:55,560
I like the way Silent Hill burns its bridges as it goes .

415
00:23:55,727 --> 00:23:58,730
I like this idea , very present in the game ,

416
00:23:58,896 --> 00:24:00,940
that there 's no going back .

417
00:24:01,107 --> 00:24:03,276
The deeper you dive into the dream ,

418
00:24:03,443 --> 00:24:06,654
the more the bridges behind you collapse .

419
00:24:07,030 --> 00:24:09,407
And this fascinates me ,

420
00:24:09,574 --> 00:24:12,994
this idea of being driven ever forward .

421
00:24:13,161 --> 00:24:14,996
Sometimes in the game

422
00:24:15,163 --> 00:24:18,166
there were places where you had to walk and walk

423
00:24:18,333 --> 00:24:19,834
for several minutes ,

424
00:24:20,001 --> 00:24:22,170
and there was no going back .

425
00:24:22,337 --> 00:24:24,297
Even if you wanted to ,

426
00:24:24,464 --> 00:24:26,632
you 'd walked so far

427
00:24:26,799 --> 00:24:29,802
that the idea of going back was exhausting .

428
00:24:29,969 --> 00:24:35,058
This is unusual in a video game . They 're usually based on speed .

429
00:24:35,224 --> 00:24:38,603
But Silent Hill had this way of taking you somewhere

430
00:24:38,770 --> 00:24:42,357
and telling you , " There 's no way back . "

431
00:24:43,691 --> 00:24:47,487
Here we have the first cut back to reality .

432
00:24:47,654 --> 00:24:50,573
I really wanted you to feel the difference .

433
00:24:50,740 --> 00:24:53,618
So I asked for really bright-coloured trucks .

434
00:24:53,785 --> 00:24:58,956
I wanted you to realise how washed-out the colours had got ,

435
00:24:59,123 --> 00:25:02,752
as we clearly pass back into a world that 's reality .

436
00:25:02,919 --> 00:25:04,837
It 's almost Technicolor .

437
00:25:05,004 --> 00:25:08,383
This is one of the scenes I added in later ,

438
00:25:08,549 --> 00:25:10,843
slightly reluctantly ,

439
00:25:11,010 --> 00:25:14,180
in order to give the audience

440
00:25:14,347 --> 00:25:17,517
a male character to relate to .

441
00:25:17,683 --> 00:25:20,019
And ... here you have him .

442
00:25:20,186 --> 00:25:22,897
These are scenes

443
00:25:23,064 --> 00:25:27,318
which are problematic for me , as I realised during the editing .

444
00:25:27,485 --> 00:25:32,907
The whole film had been thought out so that Radha Mitchell 's character

445
00:25:33,074 --> 00:25:35,618
experiences her adventure over several hours .

446
00:25:35,785 --> 00:25:38,705
But the passages back to reality

447
00:25:38,871 --> 00:25:40,998
forced me back to a world

448
00:25:41,165 --> 00:25:42,625
of normal night and day .

449
00:25:42,792 --> 00:25:47,171
This meant that the subjective time felt by the audience

450
00:25:47,338 --> 00:25:49,757
was in fact dragged out .

451
00:25:49,924 --> 00:25:55,304
As you could now see the father looking for his wife in the real world ,

452
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:57,640
over several days and nights ,

453
00:25:57,807 --> 00:26:02,061
you understood that the film 's action took place over several days .

454
00:26:02,228 --> 00:26:06,649
So this subjective time stretched out in people 's minds ,

455
00:26:06,816 --> 00:26:09,152
which I think made the film

456
00:26:09,318 --> 00:26:11,446
more contemplative . A problem .

457
00:26:11,612 --> 00:26:14,323
I kept hoping during the editing

458
00:26:14,490 --> 00:26:17,744
that TriStar would say , " The film 's too long .

459
00:26:17,910 --> 00:26:20,621
" It needs cutting . "

460
00:26:20,788 --> 00:26:24,083
My editor Sébastien Prangère and I had planned

461
00:26:25,126 --> 00:26:29,589
to cut all of these scenes . We 'd done some tests and so on ...

462
00:26:29,755 --> 00:26:33,426
We could have easily cut the scenes with Sean Bean .

463
00:26:33,593 --> 00:26:37,638
The scenes in the main body of the film , not at the start or end .

464
00:26:37,805 --> 00:26:40,558
In the end , to our great surprise ,

465
00:26:40,725 --> 00:26:44,979
the people from TriStar never came to bother us ,

466
00:26:45,146 --> 00:26:49,150
and the film was released as it was , the director 's cut ,

467
00:26:49,317 --> 00:26:54,655
without our being asked to cut it and make it a length

468
00:26:54,822 --> 00:26:58,284
that 's more normal . It 's 2 hours 7 minutes long .

469
00:26:58,451 --> 00:27:02,205
It should have been more like 1 hour 50 , or 1 hour 45 .

470
00:27:02,497 --> 00:27:06,334
The film is too long because of those unnecessary scenes .

471
00:27:07,376 --> 00:27:11,506
Radha Mitchell was , as far as I was concerned ...

472
00:27:12,131 --> 00:27:13,508
a choice

473
00:27:13,800 --> 00:27:16,719
that was very particular in so much as ...

474
00:27:16,886 --> 00:27:19,514
What I really liked about her

475
00:27:19,680 --> 00:27:22,141
was her physique ,

476
00:27:22,308 --> 00:27:25,645
which is a sort of blend of Mia Farrow and Grace Kelly .

477
00:27:25,811 --> 00:27:27,939
She looks like someone

478
00:27:28,105 --> 00:27:29,524
totally vulnerable ,

479
00:27:29,690 --> 00:27:32,235
not a superhero , just ordinary ,

480
00:27:32,401 --> 00:27:34,987
plunged into an extraordinary situation .

481
00:27:35,154 --> 00:27:37,698
I really liked that about her .

482
00:27:38,783 --> 00:27:41,035
Not to mention the fact

483
00:27:41,202 --> 00:27:43,621
that she 's a very nice person .

484
00:27:45,498 --> 00:27:50,920
Yet we didn 't have a fantastic relationship on the set .

485
00:27:51,087 --> 00:27:53,756
I think that came from a basic problem

486
00:27:53,923 --> 00:27:58,928
which was that Radha Mitchell , who was the studio 's choice ,

487
00:27:59,095 --> 00:28:01,389
TriStar 's choice ,

488
00:28:01,556 --> 00:28:03,766
rejected the film at first .

489
00:28:03,933 --> 00:28:06,060
She read the script and said no .

490
00:28:06,227 --> 00:28:08,187
In fact , she was waiting

491
00:28:08,354 --> 00:28:12,733
to see how a film she 'd just made , Melinda and Melinda , would do .

492
00:28:12,900 --> 00:28:16,487
This film , by Woody Allen , was a total flop ,

493
00:28:16,654 --> 00:28:21,325
and she came back to us and said , " Yes , I 'll do Silent Hill . "

494
00:28:21,576 --> 00:28:24,287
Due to her hesitation

495
00:28:24,453 --> 00:28:29,625
our relationship didn 't get off to the best of starts .

496
00:28:29,792 --> 00:28:32,295
It wasn 't that we hated each other

497
00:28:32,461 --> 00:28:34,422
or anything like that .

498
00:28:34,589 --> 00:28:39,343
In fact , I never had with her , nor she with me ,

499
00:28:39,510 --> 00:28:43,639
the sort of complicity such a project required .

500
00:28:44,098 --> 00:28:48,894
So it ended up being rather difficult between us .

501
00:28:49,895 --> 00:28:51,731
I regret that , quite frankly .

502
00:28:51,898 --> 00:28:55,776
She 's someone who 's very nice , very pleasant , in real life .

503
00:28:56,110 --> 00:28:59,822
I wish the two of us had hit it off better .

504
00:28:59,989 --> 00:29:02,783
But that 's often what happens with casting .

505
00:29:02,950 --> 00:29:06,829
The audience don 't usually know , lucky for them ,

506
00:29:06,996 --> 00:29:11,375
about all the false starts , the compromises

507
00:29:11,542 --> 00:29:14,670
and problems involved in choosing actors ,

508
00:29:14,837 --> 00:29:18,174
and the unfortunate psychological effects on a film set ,

509
00:29:18,341 --> 00:29:23,346
seeing as we have to live very close to each other for a certain time .

510
00:29:23,512 --> 00:29:27,683
So , when things don 't get off to the best start ,

511
00:29:27,850 --> 00:29:31,729
it can cause problems , but that 's how it is .

512
00:29:33,397 --> 00:29:37,610
So Sean Bean and Kim Coates , the police inspector ,

513
00:29:37,777 --> 00:29:40,571
will be together for several scenes .

514
00:29:40,738 --> 00:29:43,574
They 'd just worked together on The Island ,

515
00:29:43,741 --> 00:29:45,743
by Michael Bay .

516
00:29:45,910 --> 00:29:49,413
It 's a film I like a lot . It really impressed me .

517
00:29:49,580 --> 00:29:53,000
And these actors are a lot of fun .

518
00:29:53,167 --> 00:29:56,796
They act in loads of films every year .

519
00:29:56,963 --> 00:30:00,758
It 's amazing how these guys just churn them out .

520
00:30:00,925 --> 00:30:04,345
And they have their own sort of rhythm ,

521
00:30:04,512 --> 00:30:06,514
their way of working and so on .

522
00:30:06,681 --> 00:30:07,890
There 's no hesitation .

523
00:30:08,057 --> 00:30:12,645
Sometimes it 's a bit disturbing , because they don 't question anything !

524
00:30:14,230 --> 00:30:17,858
They just stand and wait for the camera to start rolling .

525
00:30:18,442 --> 00:30:22,530
They 're members of the big troupe of regular film actors .

526
00:30:22,697 --> 00:30:24,782
And they 're very nice people .

527
00:30:25,199 --> 00:30:29,453
Laurie Holden , who plays Cybil ,

528
00:30:29,870 --> 00:30:31,247
the policewoman ,

529
00:30:31,414 --> 00:30:35,543
was someone I 'd noticed in The Majestic ,

530
00:30:35,710 --> 00:30:38,254
the film with Jim Carrey ,

531
00:30:38,421 --> 00:30:39,714
a film I loved ,

532
00:30:39,880 --> 00:30:44,260
but which got absolutely slaughtered when it was released .

533
00:30:44,427 --> 00:30:47,555
This poor film , which was about McCarthyism ,

534
00:30:47,722 --> 00:30:50,850
had the bad luck to be released just after 9 / 11 .

535
00:30:51,016 --> 00:30:56,480
So America , going through a phase of intense patriotism and solidarity ,

536
00:30:56,647 --> 00:31:01,902
was confronted with this film about one of America 's darkest periods ,

537
00:31:02,069 --> 00:31:04,613
so the film was shit on !

538
00:31:04,780 --> 00:31:08,284
And Laurie Holden , who had the main female role ,

539
00:31:08,451 --> 00:31:10,703
and who was very good ,

540
00:31:10,870 --> 00:31:14,790
was in a way robbed of her springboard to success .

541
00:31:17,418 --> 00:31:21,422
So I chose her for Silent Hill both because I thought she was good

542
00:31:21,589 --> 00:31:23,507
in Darabont 's film ,

543
00:31:23,674 --> 00:31:27,720
but also because I thought she had a Western-style attitude .

544
00:31:27,887 --> 00:31:29,930
Her physique ...

545
00:31:30,097 --> 00:31:33,059
Her slim hips give her a way of moving

546
00:31:33,225 --> 00:31:35,978
that 's very " Western " , very masculine .

547
00:31:36,145 --> 00:31:39,523
I thought she 'd be fantastic as a policewoman ,

548
00:31:39,690 --> 00:31:42,526
in the tight leather trousers and so on .

549
00:31:42,693 --> 00:31:46,447
And I think I was right . The character is pretty iconic .

550
00:31:46,614 --> 00:31:48,741
But she 's an actress

551
00:31:48,908 --> 00:31:52,870
who got really involved in the shooting of the film .

552
00:31:53,037 --> 00:31:55,539
Even a little too much at times .

553
00:31:55,873 --> 00:31:59,251
She 's under pressure the whole time she 's on the set .

554
00:31:59,877 --> 00:32:03,672
She 's totally obsessed with ...

555
00:32:03,839 --> 00:32:06,050
with being on the set ,

556
00:32:06,217 --> 00:32:09,804
with getting completely in character and so on .

557
00:32:09,970 --> 00:32:13,265
This sometimes caused problems with Radha Mitchell ,

558
00:32:13,432 --> 00:32:15,643
who is slower to get going .

559
00:32:15,810 --> 00:32:20,105
Radha Mitchell reaches cruising speed by the sixth take ,

560
00:32:20,272 --> 00:32:24,026
whereas Laurie Holden is full-on in the first .

561
00:32:26,821 --> 00:32:30,199
This made working with them both very complicated .

562
00:32:30,366 --> 00:32:35,412
There were some tricky moments for me , when they weren 't in synch ,

563
00:32:35,579 --> 00:32:38,207
because of their different ways of working .

564
00:32:38,666 --> 00:32:42,002
So it was quite difficult , at times ,

565
00:32:42,169 --> 00:32:45,172
to find an harmony on the set .

566
00:32:45,339 --> 00:32:47,800
The Armless was played by a hip-hop dancer .

567
00:32:49,093 --> 00:32:51,053
Every monster was played

568
00:32:51,220 --> 00:32:53,472
by a dancer with a particular style .

569
00:32:53,639 --> 00:32:56,350
He was totally blind inside that suit .

570
00:32:56,517 --> 00:32:58,936
As were most of them .

571
00:32:59,103 --> 00:33:02,523
They were all played by actors who had no way of seeing

572
00:33:02,690 --> 00:33:04,942
any of the set around them .

573
00:33:05,109 --> 00:33:08,112
So working with them was rather unusual .

574
00:33:08,279 --> 00:33:10,197
It was very tough on them .

575
00:33:10,364 --> 00:33:13,909
It was boiling hot on the sets . Especially this one .

576
00:33:14,076 --> 00:33:17,997
This stretch of road had been re-created

577
00:33:18,164 --> 00:33:22,376
in a big hangar , not a studio , so there was no air-conditioning .

578
00:33:22,543 --> 00:33:25,671
And that , added to the chemical atmosphere

579
00:33:25,838 --> 00:33:29,466
given off by the steam which made the fog ,

580
00:33:29,633 --> 00:33:31,093
made it unbearable .

581
00:33:31,260 --> 00:33:35,181
On top of which , that poor guy was wrapped up in his suit .

582
00:33:35,347 --> 00:33:38,517
I think he must have suffered horribly .

583
00:33:39,101 --> 00:33:41,979
So I 'm paying homage to him today .

584
00:33:42,146 --> 00:33:44,815
Sometimes , when you 're writing these things ,

585
00:33:44,982 --> 00:33:49,987
you don 't always realise what you 'll be inflicting on people .

586
00:33:51,280 --> 00:33:54,074
This was an idea I was very pleased with .

587
00:33:54,241 --> 00:33:57,912
In video games , of course , you can click onto a map ,

588
00:33:58,078 --> 00:34:00,247
to find your bearings .

589
00:34:00,414 --> 00:34:03,584
That 's not the case in a film , obviously ,

590
00:34:03,751 --> 00:34:06,211
so I thought that , to find her way ,

591
00:34:06,378 --> 00:34:10,382
the character would follow the bus stops .

592
00:34:10,549 --> 00:34:12,718
It 's her way of finding her bearings .

593
00:34:13,093 --> 00:34:16,013
It refers to something that happened to me .

594
00:34:16,180 --> 00:34:18,766
I once got lost in Milan , Italy .

595
00:34:19,391 --> 00:34:22,227
And to find my way I followed bus stops .

596
00:34:22,394 --> 00:34:24,563
So that 's how I thought up

597
00:34:24,730 --> 00:34:28,442
how she 'd find her way around at Silent Hill .

598
00:34:28,984 --> 00:34:32,321
One thing that 's very important in Silent Hill ,

599
00:34:32,488 --> 00:34:34,281
taken straight from the game ,

600
00:34:34,448 --> 00:34:36,867
is that there are messages everywhere ,

601
00:34:37,034 --> 00:34:38,911
things you have to read ,

602
00:34:39,078 --> 00:34:41,580
to understand the story behind the story .

603
00:34:41,956 --> 00:34:44,917
To understand where it is you 're going .

604
00:34:45,084 --> 00:34:49,171
And in this school there are messages ,

605
00:34:50,256 --> 00:34:53,133
clues all over the place ,

606
00:34:53,300 --> 00:34:57,429
which give the audience hints about the past of Silent Hill .

607
00:34:57,596 --> 00:35:00,140
Obviously , one of the things about video games

608
00:35:00,307 --> 00:35:03,686
is that it 's risky to slow them down with explanations .

609
00:35:04,520 --> 00:35:07,523
Game creators are reticent about doing that .

610
00:35:07,690 --> 00:35:10,192
The way they envisage the narrative

611
00:35:10,359 --> 00:35:14,238
is to leave it , to some extent , up to the player 's imagination .

612
00:35:14,405 --> 00:35:15,489
The player plays ,

613
00:35:15,656 --> 00:35:20,119
but thanks to these clues , phrases and notices

614
00:35:20,286 --> 00:35:21,662
all over the place ,

615
00:35:21,829 --> 00:35:26,500
is able to form an idea of the story behind the story .

616
00:35:27,084 --> 00:35:30,587
That 's one of the most direct borrowings from the game .

617
00:35:31,255 --> 00:35:35,092
It 's something you hardly ever get , I think , in films :

618
00:35:35,259 --> 00:35:37,886
simply suggesting to the audience that ...

619
00:35:38,053 --> 00:35:41,056
There 's something there to be understood ,

620
00:35:41,223 --> 00:35:43,976
and they can either choose to understand it ,

621
00:35:44,143 --> 00:35:48,981
or just pass it by and ignore that level of the film .

622
00:35:49,398 --> 00:35:53,027
That was one of the things that was the most ...

623
00:35:54,486 --> 00:35:58,615
the most radically inspired by video games , in the whole film ,

624
00:35:58,782 --> 00:36:02,286
and the most risky , but I think it was worth a try ,

625
00:36:02,453 --> 00:36:06,498
considering the importance of video-game culture today .

626
00:36:06,665 --> 00:36:09,376
I thought it was interesting for a film to try

627
00:36:09,543 --> 00:36:13,297
not just to adapt the obvious video-game stuff ,

628
00:36:13,464 --> 00:36:17,134
the kinetic aspect , the aesthetics , the action ,

629
00:36:17,301 --> 00:36:19,887
but also to try to get across

630
00:36:20,054 --> 00:36:22,264
the weird narrative .

631
00:36:22,431 --> 00:36:25,934
To invite that into a film and see what the result is .

632
00:36:26,101 --> 00:36:29,897
I 'm not saying I achieved it , that it was a total success .

633
00:36:30,064 --> 00:36:33,901
But I think it was worth trying it and saying ,

634
00:36:34,068 --> 00:36:36,779
" We 're adapting a video game but it won 't be ... "

635
00:36:36,945 --> 00:36:41,241
For most people , a video game is just people shooting at each other .

636
00:36:41,408 --> 00:36:44,161
Speaking as someone who loves video games ,

637
00:36:44,328 --> 00:36:47,331
I think video games have their own language .

638
00:36:47,539 --> 00:36:52,586
And it interested me to introduce some of that language into a film .

639
00:36:54,838 --> 00:36:59,843
This definition of Silent Hill as a mining town ,

640
00:37:00,010 --> 00:37:02,679
which had suffered a catastrophe ,

641
00:37:02,846 --> 00:37:05,766
with the mines igniting and making the town

642
00:37:05,933 --> 00:37:07,393
impossible to live in ,

643
00:37:07,559 --> 00:37:12,106
was a contribution of Roger Avary , the scriptwriter .

644
00:37:12,356 --> 00:37:17,361
He was fascinated to hear about a mining town like this ,

645
00:37:17,528 --> 00:37:21,657
which really exists but isn 't called Silent Hill , of course .

646
00:37:21,824 --> 00:37:24,576
And he thought it was a neat way

647
00:37:25,119 --> 00:37:29,289
of explaining why the town was in a sort of perpetual fog ,

648
00:37:29,456 --> 00:37:31,542
with ash raining down .

649
00:37:32,126 --> 00:37:36,505
As well as the symbolic dimension , he wanted this rational explanation ,

650
00:37:36,672 --> 00:37:39,425
which I thought was a very good idea ,

651
00:37:39,591 --> 00:37:43,053
that the town 's climate , its atmosphere ,

652
00:37:43,220 --> 00:37:47,015
could be explained in several ways , including this one .

653
00:37:47,975 --> 00:37:52,187
In the game there were silhouettes that came back regularly ,

654
00:37:52,354 --> 00:37:55,858
the silhouettes of people in boiler suits .

655
00:37:56,024 --> 00:37:57,860
They weren 't explained ,

656
00:37:58,026 --> 00:38:00,362
and when we brought in this idea

657
00:38:00,529 --> 00:38:05,409
that the town was a mining town built on a system of tunnels ,

658
00:38:05,909 --> 00:38:08,078
it suddenly seemed obvious

659
00:38:08,245 --> 00:38:12,624
to make these silhouettes in overalls miners .

660
00:38:12,791 --> 00:38:16,503
We imagined that the people still living in Silent Hill

661
00:38:16,670 --> 00:38:19,131
put these suits on

662
00:38:19,298 --> 00:38:23,135
to go into the town and possibly confront the creatures

663
00:38:23,302 --> 00:38:25,804
that jump on you when darkness falls .

664
00:38:27,639 --> 00:38:30,601
The heroine has just run over a hopscotch game ,

665
00:38:31,935 --> 00:38:34,146
with " heaven " and " hell " written

666
00:38:34,313 --> 00:38:37,399
in the squares at either end .

667
00:38:37,691 --> 00:38:40,360
It 's typical of this idea of the fantastic ,

668
00:38:40,527 --> 00:38:44,114
that everything that 's evil or diabolical

669
00:38:44,281 --> 00:38:47,576
lurks around the world of childhood ,

670
00:38:47,743 --> 00:38:52,372
and that what looks like a game is the sign of an evil power ,

671
00:38:54,541 --> 00:38:57,878
which first and foremost attacks the purity of childhood .

672
00:38:58,045 --> 00:39:00,631
It 's something that you find

673
00:39:00,797 --> 00:39:04,509
in American horror and fantasy , which is in essence Puritan ,

674
00:39:04,676 --> 00:39:07,054
and in Japanese horror and fantasy ,

675
00:39:07,221 --> 00:39:12,226
which takes great pains to define purity .

676
00:39:12,392 --> 00:39:14,394
And the purity of children

677
00:39:14,561 --> 00:39:17,814
is an undeniable source for Japanese horror and fantasy .

678
00:39:17,981 --> 00:39:21,526
And I think this is one of the bridges

679
00:39:21,693 --> 00:39:25,906
between these two folk traditions of envisaging the fantastic .

680
00:39:27,616 --> 00:39:30,202
Here again , we 're back to following clues .

681
00:39:30,369 --> 00:39:34,331
These signs which help the heroine understand things .

682
00:39:34,498 --> 00:39:37,793
Here , it 's a child 's handmarks in the dust on a desk .

683
00:39:39,461 --> 00:39:42,214
That 's typical of the way video games work .

684
00:39:42,381 --> 00:39:45,801
You walk into an empty room , with nothing apparently happening ,

685
00:39:45,968 --> 00:39:47,678
and you have to find the clue

686
00:39:47,844 --> 00:39:50,931
which will let you progress to the next stage .

687
00:39:52,724 --> 00:39:54,351
The chase in the school

688
00:39:54,518 --> 00:39:57,980
is one of the very rare references to a famous film .

689
00:39:58,772 --> 00:40:03,110
I was a bit annoyed with myself for doing it , but I couldn 't help it .

690
00:40:03,485 --> 00:40:05,904
It 's one of my favourite films .

691
00:40:06,071 --> 00:40:10,867
It 's borrowed from a scene in a film by Mario Bava : Operazione paura ,

692
00:40:11,034 --> 00:40:13,370
or Kill , Baby ... Kill ! .

693
00:40:13,620 --> 00:40:17,416
One of my favourite horror and fantasy directors , Mario Bava ,

694
00:40:17,582 --> 00:40:19,418
an Italian director of the 60s ,

695
00:40:19,584 --> 00:40:23,088
who really favoured this sort of atmosphere ,

696
00:40:23,255 --> 00:40:25,716
of decomposition and ...

697
00:40:27,342 --> 00:40:31,179
empty buildings with a glorious past ,

698
00:40:31,346 --> 00:40:35,726
but which have now been corrupted by the damp and by time .

699
00:40:37,311 --> 00:40:41,481
For a horror and fantasy style it 's almost literary , melancholic ...

700
00:40:42,441 --> 00:40:46,486
It 's melancholic the way the film takes us through these places ,

701
00:40:46,653 --> 00:40:50,782
which have known their share of joy , but of tragedy too ,

702
00:40:52,242 --> 00:40:54,536
but which are now just empty shells ,

703
00:40:54,703 --> 00:40:57,748
inhabited by shadows , the shadows of the past .

704
00:40:57,914 --> 00:41:01,084
That 's quite an old definition of the fantastic ,

705
00:41:01,251 --> 00:41:03,462
a romantic definition .

706
00:41:03,629 --> 00:41:05,505
And I think that the film

707
00:41:05,672 --> 00:41:08,467
clearly tries to have that mood .

708
00:41:08,717 --> 00:41:10,636
In this scene , originally ,

709
00:41:10,802 --> 00:41:13,930
there was nothing in the last cubicle .

710
00:41:14,097 --> 00:41:17,893
You just heard the girl crying and then the heroine ...

711
00:41:18,060 --> 00:41:21,146
opened the cubicle door and there was nothing .

712
00:41:21,313 --> 00:41:23,023
That was the surprise .

713
00:41:24,483 --> 00:41:27,611
Then I thought , " It 's a shame there 's nothing ! "

714
00:41:28,236 --> 00:41:30,280
And as we went on ,

715
00:41:31,365 --> 00:41:34,785
I imagined a creature that isn 't in the game ,

716
00:41:35,202 --> 00:41:38,163
a creature I designed pretty quickly ,

717
00:41:38,330 --> 00:41:41,124
a creature which also refers

718
00:41:41,291 --> 00:41:44,419
to a very popular culture in Japan ,

719
00:41:44,586 --> 00:41:48,090
which is that of bondage , of sado-masochism .

720
00:41:48,256 --> 00:41:52,761
So I imagined this creature , tortured with barbed wire ,

721
00:41:52,928 --> 00:41:57,099
twisted into an impossible position , which then goes after the heroine .

722
00:41:57,265 --> 00:42:01,561
First it seems to be a body , then it 's a creature , which haunts ...

723
00:42:02,521 --> 00:42:04,272
these toilets .

724
00:42:05,399 --> 00:42:08,527
It 's a creature that was created at the last minute , if you like .

725
00:42:08,694 --> 00:42:11,863
It was on the set that we thought it up .

726
00:42:12,030 --> 00:42:14,408
It was the film 's choreographer ,

727
00:42:14,574 --> 00:42:18,286
who worked with all the dancers who played the monsters ,

728
00:42:18,453 --> 00:42:19,955
who played this monster .

729
00:42:20,122 --> 00:42:24,710
As well as playing Red Pyramid , he also played this creature .

730
00:42:24,876 --> 00:42:26,837
His name 's Roberto Campanella .

731
00:42:27,003 --> 00:42:31,133
And he 's largely responsible for the success of the film 's creatures .

732
00:42:32,134 --> 00:42:35,095
The writing above the body suggests

733
00:42:35,262 --> 00:42:37,931
- and this makes it more horrible -

734
00:42:38,098 --> 00:42:41,977
that the torture inflicted on this man

735
00:42:42,144 --> 00:42:45,981
was done in the spirit of a children 's game .

736
00:42:47,023 --> 00:42:49,151
Here we 're getting to the core

737
00:42:49,860 --> 00:42:52,696
of the definition of the town Silent Hill ,

738
00:42:52,863 --> 00:42:55,824
which is that it 's a tormented , tortured place .

739
00:42:55,991 --> 00:42:58,410
The reality of this little town

740
00:42:58,577 --> 00:43:00,704
has been , in a way , tortured

741
00:43:00,996 --> 00:43:02,205
by a child .

742
00:43:02,372 --> 00:43:06,710
Like a little girl using a lighter to melt her doll .

743
00:43:07,419 --> 00:43:08,086
Well ,

744
00:43:08,295 --> 00:43:11,965
the character of Alessa has done the same to an entire town ,

745
00:43:12,132 --> 00:43:14,301
held a flame to it , in a way .

746
00:43:15,427 --> 00:43:18,430
The whole film displays the signs of this torment .

747
00:43:20,807 --> 00:43:22,350
What I like about this idea

748
00:43:23,226 --> 00:43:27,606
is that the body of the town isn 't so much symbolised by its inhabitants ,

749
00:43:27,773 --> 00:43:29,316
as by its architecture .

750
00:43:29,483 --> 00:43:31,401
It 's the idea

751
00:43:32,360 --> 00:43:34,696
that the town has a real living body ,

752
00:43:34,863 --> 00:43:38,700
that this body is the buildings , the alleyways and so on ,

753
00:43:38,867 --> 00:43:41,745
and that these places can be tortured

754
00:43:41,912 --> 00:43:45,040
just like a body of flesh and bone can be .

755
00:43:45,207 --> 00:43:50,003
It 's an idea that 's found , obviously , in modern art ,

756
00:43:50,337 --> 00:43:54,800
an idea that 's been worked on a lot , that the appearances around us

757
00:43:54,966 --> 00:43:58,678
can be , in a way , damaged and torn to shreds

758
00:43:59,805 --> 00:44:00,889
by the artist .

759
00:44:01,056 --> 00:44:02,849
And to me , Alessa

760
00:44:03,016 --> 00:44:06,520
- and this is something I felt deeply when playing the game -

761
00:44:06,686 --> 00:44:09,481
is an artist , a tormented artist ,

762
00:44:09,648 --> 00:44:14,069
who , in a way , imposes her torture on reality .

763
00:44:14,236 --> 00:44:16,863
Not just on the people living in this town ,

764
00:44:17,030 --> 00:44:19,699
but also on its entire environment .

765
00:44:19,866 --> 00:44:23,662
To me , that 's a fascinating and more or less unique idea .

766
00:44:23,829 --> 00:44:27,374
This scene illustrates what I was saying

767
00:44:27,541 --> 00:44:30,710
about this spirit which , in a way , haunts Silent Hill ,

768
00:44:31,294 --> 00:44:34,631
which tortures the surroundings like you 'd torture a body .

769
00:44:36,383 --> 00:44:40,637
It was BUF , the famous French visual effects studio ,

770
00:44:41,096 --> 00:44:45,267
which did the scenes where the town undergoes these transformations .

771
00:44:45,433 --> 00:44:47,686
We spent a lot of time

772
00:44:48,311 --> 00:44:53,275
discussing the process , and getting an effect whereby the ...

773
00:44:54,109 --> 00:44:57,904
the decor is transformed like flesh that 's being burnt ,

774
00:44:58,238 --> 00:45:00,282
with a flame held to it .

775
00:45:01,449 --> 00:45:04,160
I think the result is quite amazing .

776
00:45:04,786 --> 00:45:07,706
This idea wasn 't explored so much in the game ,

777
00:45:07,873 --> 00:45:12,335
where the character usually fainted and woke up in another world .

778
00:45:12,836 --> 00:45:15,714
Here I wanted this effect of decrepitude ,

779
00:45:17,549 --> 00:45:18,675
which is very particular ,

780
00:45:19,718 --> 00:45:23,555
underscored by very organic sounds , created by Nicolas Becker ,

781
00:45:23,722 --> 00:45:26,182
our sound designer ,

782
00:45:26,349 --> 00:45:29,394
to get this effect of the decor suddenly becoming

783
00:45:29,561 --> 00:45:34,441
like an orifice , an orifice of flesh subjected to fire .

784
00:45:36,026 --> 00:45:37,777
It 's an idea of torture ,

785
00:45:37,944 --> 00:45:40,572
which will , later in the film ,

786
00:45:40,739 --> 00:45:43,617
be directly applied to one of the characters .

787
00:45:44,826 --> 00:45:49,581
The shots you see in the film , from unusual angles or positions ,

788
00:45:50,373 --> 00:45:53,168
were made possible

789
00:45:53,335 --> 00:45:56,504
because a lot of the sets were totally constructed .

790
00:45:56,755 --> 00:45:59,215
They were not only built in the studio ,

791
00:45:59,382 --> 00:46:01,760
but to some extent they were designed

792
00:46:01,926 --> 00:46:05,722
to let the camera get shots at angles I 'd envisaged

793
00:46:05,889 --> 00:46:07,515
on the storyboards .

794
00:46:07,682 --> 00:46:10,352
So a lot of the sets were detachable ,

795
00:46:10,518 --> 00:46:15,106
a lot of the elements of the sets were specially worked

796
00:46:15,273 --> 00:46:17,984
to make certain shots possible .

797
00:46:18,151 --> 00:46:19,986
One of the film 's complexities

798
00:46:20,153 --> 00:46:22,864
was that the angles were so specific

799
00:46:23,031 --> 00:46:25,659
that we were at times forced to film

800
00:46:25,825 --> 00:46:27,994
in a dislocated way ,

801
00:46:28,161 --> 00:46:32,082
doing one angle , another and another , until we got a complete scene .

802
00:46:32,248 --> 00:46:34,125
That 's very complicated ,

803
00:46:34,292 --> 00:46:37,671
and it made everyone on the production very tense .

804
00:46:37,837 --> 00:46:42,550
especially the producer , a Canadian called Don Carmody .

805
00:46:42,926 --> 00:46:46,096
This way of filming made him very tense .

806
00:46:46,262 --> 00:46:50,100
He was used to being on the sets of B movies ,

807
00:46:50,266 --> 00:46:53,520
and movies which get filmed quite straightforwardly ,

808
00:46:53,687 --> 00:46:58,274
and the fact that this film was so complicated and precise

809
00:46:58,441 --> 00:47:00,986
made him rather tense .

810
00:47:02,487 --> 00:47:05,281
Here we see the first encounter

811
00:47:05,448 --> 00:47:07,450
between Radha Mitchell and ...

812
00:47:08,410 --> 00:47:09,744
the Alessa character ,

813
00:47:09,911 --> 00:47:14,082
who 's the double , the original , of her daughter ,

814
00:47:14,708 --> 00:47:18,294
and therefore the brains

815
00:47:18,461 --> 00:47:20,839
behind the mystery of Silent Hill .

816
00:47:21,423 --> 00:47:22,966
In fact , we 're in her head .

817
00:47:23,133 --> 00:47:26,928
She 's drawing what we can see happening .

818
00:47:27,095 --> 00:47:32,142
The idea is that she wildly scribbles a drawing

819
00:47:32,308 --> 00:47:36,438
in the same way as she tortures the decor .

820
00:47:36,604 --> 00:47:39,441
It 's a massacre with crayons .

821
00:47:39,607 --> 00:47:42,235
So the idea , at this point ,

822
00:47:42,402 --> 00:47:46,114
is that we not only see the decor , but its architect too .

823
00:47:46,740 --> 00:47:49,951
And the architect , as it happens , is this little girl .

824
00:47:50,118 --> 00:47:55,123
I think this sequence in the school is the most spectacular in the film

825
00:47:55,290 --> 00:47:57,667
and closest to the game .

826
00:47:57,834 --> 00:48:01,087
If the people listening have never played the game ,

827
00:48:01,254 --> 00:48:05,925
this will give them a good idea of its rather special atmosphere ,

828
00:48:06,092 --> 00:48:09,637
which made it famous , even though it didn 't sell much .

829
00:48:09,804 --> 00:48:13,308
I was surprised to see the sales figures for France ,

830
00:48:13,475 --> 00:48:15,643
which were only in their hundreds .

831
00:48:15,810 --> 00:48:18,063
It 's a very particular game .

832
00:48:18,229 --> 00:48:20,774
It 's very hard to tolerate

833
00:48:20,940 --> 00:48:23,985
an atmosphere like this for hours on end .

834
00:48:24,152 --> 00:48:25,862
And few people try .

835
00:48:26,029 --> 00:48:30,366
The game has a huge reputation , even outside gaming circles ,

836
00:48:30,533 --> 00:48:34,079
but if you do your sums , few have actually played it .

837
00:48:34,245 --> 00:48:35,080
Odd !

838
00:48:35,246 --> 00:48:37,624
This scene in the school playground

839
00:48:37,791 --> 00:48:41,085
introduces one of the most emblematic characters

840
00:48:41,252 --> 00:48:43,379
in the history of video games ,

841
00:48:43,546 --> 00:48:45,715
the Red Pyramid character ,

842
00:48:45,882 --> 00:48:47,675
this sort of executioner .

843
00:48:47,842 --> 00:48:51,012
I think he looks like one of those penitents ...

844
00:48:51,179 --> 00:48:52,764
those penitents in black

845
00:48:52,931 --> 00:48:54,808
that you got in Spain ,

846
00:48:54,974 --> 00:48:58,061
linked to the history of the Inquisition and so on .

847
00:48:58,228 --> 00:49:00,271
In the game he 's an executioner ,

848
00:49:00,438 --> 00:49:03,650
linked to the male character

849
00:49:03,817 --> 00:49:07,403
of the second game , Silent Hill 2 .

850
00:49:21,459 --> 00:49:27,215
Here , the wife brushes by her husband in a parallel dimension ,

851
00:49:27,382 --> 00:49:30,510
and this shows us how the universe of Silent Hill works ,

852
00:49:30,677 --> 00:49:34,013
by the superimposition of parallel dimensions .

853
00:49:34,472 --> 00:49:38,059
It 's my favourite scene of all those with Sean Bean ,

854
00:49:38,226 --> 00:49:39,435
the husband .

855
00:49:39,602 --> 00:49:42,480
And it 's true that this scene

856
00:49:42,647 --> 00:49:45,525
allowed us to say

857
00:49:45,692 --> 00:49:48,820
that the scenes with Sean Bean were useful to the film .

858
00:49:50,488 --> 00:49:52,407
It is a nice sequence ,

859
00:49:52,574 --> 00:49:55,660
with a clear reference to the myth of Orpheus ,

860
00:49:55,827 --> 00:49:58,955
which was given a film treatment by Jean Cocteau .

861
00:49:59,122 --> 00:50:04,335
But was it worth slowing down the whole film just for this scene ?

862
00:50:04,502 --> 00:50:08,631
I don 't know . But by itself the scene works very well .

863
00:50:14,095 --> 00:50:17,098
In the game the cockroaches weren 't that great .

864
00:50:17,265 --> 00:50:20,727
They 'd swarm round your feet and nibble at you ,

865
00:50:20,894 --> 00:50:23,146
reducing your life bar .

866
00:50:23,521 --> 00:50:26,107
They had no more function than that ,

867
00:50:26,274 --> 00:50:28,776
they just increased the stress

868
00:50:28,943 --> 00:50:29,861
for the player .

869
00:50:30,028 --> 00:50:33,573
We tried to give them a bit more personality ,

870
00:50:33,740 --> 00:50:36,576
making them a living mass , like a marabunta ,

871
00:50:36,743 --> 00:50:40,121
a column of soldier ants which eats everything in its path .

872
00:50:40,288 --> 00:50:42,332
But also , in this passage ,

873
00:50:42,498 --> 00:50:45,210
we discover that these cockroaches

874
00:50:45,376 --> 00:50:48,213
used to be human beings .

875
00:50:48,379 --> 00:50:51,007
They are some of the lost souls

876
00:50:51,174 --> 00:50:53,593
stuck in the hell of Silent Hill .

877
00:50:57,597 --> 00:51:00,350
The two heroines are stuck in the boiler room ,

878
00:51:00,516 --> 00:51:03,353
where they 'll be attacked by Red Pyramid ,

879
00:51:03,519 --> 00:51:06,731
who 's going to pierce the door with his huge sword .

880
00:51:07,190 --> 00:51:10,985
And this is one of the many scenes

881
00:51:12,362 --> 00:51:15,782
in which the film tackles the idea of rape .

882
00:51:16,241 --> 00:51:18,952
Rape inflicted directly on the set .

883
00:51:19,118 --> 00:51:22,372
This place where the two women have holed up ,

884
00:51:22,538 --> 00:51:25,458
and the way in which it is split open

885
00:51:25,625 --> 00:51:28,920
by Red Pyramid 's enormous sword ...

886
00:51:29,087 --> 00:51:33,508
This is a way of inflicting on the decor something ...

887
00:51:33,675 --> 00:51:38,721
which , of course , would usually be inflicted on a human body .

888
00:51:40,056 --> 00:51:43,101
This idea recurs regularly in the film ,

889
00:51:43,267 --> 00:51:44,560
the idea that ...

890
00:51:45,520 --> 00:51:50,191
the set suffers the abuse on behalf of the characters .

891
00:51:57,949 --> 00:51:59,617
I like the idea

892
00:51:59,784 --> 00:52:02,495
that the ' roaches are like the sperm here ,

893
00:52:02,662 --> 00:52:05,707
pouring in through the broken door

894
00:52:05,873 --> 00:52:07,417
and invading the room .

895
00:52:08,042 --> 00:52:10,211
I think it 's all part ...

896
00:52:10,670 --> 00:52:15,133
of what creates the unease , of course , in this universe .

897
00:52:15,967 --> 00:52:18,803
But it 's also a symbolic interpretation ,

898
00:52:18,970 --> 00:52:21,764
which you find in all modern art ,

899
00:52:21,931 --> 00:52:25,435
this way of transcribing anxieties and unpleasant things

900
00:52:25,601 --> 00:52:28,187
by playing around with the symbols .

901
00:52:29,022 --> 00:52:32,066
It 's something I tried to keep in mind .

902
00:52:32,233 --> 00:52:34,360
Maybe it 's one reason why the film

903
00:52:34,861 --> 00:52:38,573
didn 't take a real hammering from the censors .

904
00:52:38,781 --> 00:52:40,283
We expected the film

905
00:52:41,409 --> 00:52:44,620
to be given an 18 rating in England , a 16 in France ...

906
00:52:46,080 --> 00:52:50,835
But the film got past all the censors without any real problem .

907
00:52:51,085 --> 00:52:54,213
I think that it 's so overtly symbolic

908
00:52:54,380 --> 00:52:57,884
that , in the end , the censors had nothing to say .

909
00:52:58,051 --> 00:53:00,511
Everything 's a matter of interpretation ,

910
00:53:01,179 --> 00:53:03,723
so it was easy to not understand .

911
00:53:03,890 --> 00:53:07,477
I think this is one reason the film was spared

912
00:53:07,643 --> 00:53:11,147
what happens to a lot of horror films nowadays ,

913
00:53:11,314 --> 00:53:13,524
getting unjustly banned .

914
00:53:16,778 --> 00:53:21,324
This is one of the most precise visions

915
00:53:21,491 --> 00:53:24,827
of what happens when you pass from one dimension to another .

916
00:53:24,994 --> 00:53:27,038
The ash

917
00:53:27,205 --> 00:53:30,792
and the debris of one dimension

918
00:53:30,958 --> 00:53:33,336
form the skin of another .

919
00:53:33,503 --> 00:53:37,006
I like the way the BUF technicians did this .

920
00:53:37,173 --> 00:53:40,635
They gave it a very Jean Cocteau aspect .

921
00:53:40,802 --> 00:53:43,388
The world takes shape again

922
00:53:43,554 --> 00:53:47,600
as if it had been filmed and the film run backwards .

923
00:53:47,767 --> 00:53:52,105
The pieces of reality stick together again

924
00:53:53,356 --> 00:53:54,816
like film running backwards .

925
00:53:54,982 --> 00:53:56,943
I think it 's a lovely idea ,

926
00:53:57,110 --> 00:53:59,862
which works very well in this world .

927
00:54:04,534 --> 00:54:09,580
This part here ... We 're exactly halfway through the film ,

928
00:54:09,747 --> 00:54:12,583
and it 's the moment where the film leaves

929
00:54:12,750 --> 00:54:17,338
the very simple narrative

930
00:54:17,505 --> 00:54:19,882
of the video game , following clues ,

931
00:54:20,049 --> 00:54:22,844
to embark on a more complex narrative

932
00:54:23,010 --> 00:54:26,681
which tries to explain what really happened at Silent Hill .

933
00:54:26,848 --> 00:54:30,476
So now we get the intervention of other characters ,

934
00:54:30,643 --> 00:54:34,605
and we 're going to discover the key to the story .

935
00:54:34,939 --> 00:54:38,109
This was a risky thing to do .

936
00:54:38,484 --> 00:54:41,946
I still wonder whether it was the right thing to do .

937
00:54:42,113 --> 00:54:45,992
Couldn 't I have tried to keep the film going

938
00:54:46,159 --> 00:54:49,120
in the almost abstract manner of the first half ,

939
00:54:49,287 --> 00:54:53,624
and not give a more conventional structure to the second ?

940
00:54:53,791 --> 00:54:56,711
But would they have let me make such a film ?

941
00:54:56,878 --> 00:55:00,006
These are questions I 'll never have answers to .

942
00:55:00,173 --> 00:55:03,759
But I still wonder whether it was a good thing to do ,

943
00:55:03,926 --> 00:55:06,637
to make the second half more classic ,

944
00:55:06,804 --> 00:55:10,474
and to bring in so many characters .

945
00:55:12,560 --> 00:55:16,189
This little scene with Sean Bean in his car

946
00:55:16,355 --> 00:55:20,776
was shot during the re-shooting of the film .

947
00:55:20,943 --> 00:55:22,987
We finished the film , edited it ,

948
00:55:23,154 --> 00:55:26,741
and then , several months later , we did the re-shooting .

949
00:55:26,908 --> 00:55:30,620
This scene replaces a clumsier one ,

950
00:55:30,786 --> 00:55:33,122
which took place in the town library ,

951
00:55:33,289 --> 00:55:38,544
where the character looked for clues about what had happened at Silent Hill .

952
00:55:38,711 --> 00:55:40,880
We 'd imagined

953
00:55:41,047 --> 00:55:42,840
that he would break ...

954
00:55:43,007 --> 00:55:46,219
that he 'd break into the library at night

955
00:55:46,385 --> 00:55:49,305
and consult the documentation himself .

956
00:55:49,472 --> 00:55:53,309
That 's just one of the little things we changed

957
00:55:53,476 --> 00:55:58,189
between the original shooting and the final result .

958
00:55:58,481 --> 00:56:01,984
The film was shot in an extremely short timeframe ,

959
00:56:02,151 --> 00:56:05,029
in 61 days , to be precise .

960
00:56:05,196 --> 00:56:07,698
That 's quite short when you consider

961
00:56:07,865 --> 00:56:12,453
that there are over 110 different sets in the film .

962
00:56:12,620 --> 00:56:15,915
So some days I had to shoot three sets in the day .

963
00:56:16,082 --> 00:56:17,250
That was complicated .

964
00:56:17,416 --> 00:56:20,378
One in the morning , one in the afternoon , OK ,

965
00:56:20,544 --> 00:56:24,590
but when you have to do three sets , three different scenes ,

966
00:56:24,757 --> 00:56:26,968
with people changing ,

967
00:56:27,134 --> 00:56:30,304
the lighting to set up and so on , it 's complicated .

968
00:56:31,138 --> 00:56:33,724
This explains the film 's style , to some extent .

969
00:56:33,891 --> 00:56:36,352
It 's different from Brotherhood of the Wolf ,

970
00:56:36,519 --> 00:56:37,895
with fewer shots ,

971
00:56:38,062 --> 00:56:39,897
the shots are more streamlined ,

972
00:56:40,648 --> 00:56:44,360
and try to encompass the set immediately .

973
00:56:46,696 --> 00:56:50,866
The film was quite hard to make because of this schedule , but ...

974
00:56:51,284 --> 00:56:53,077
At the same time ,

975
00:56:53,244 --> 00:56:56,372
its budget was more than comfortable for a horror film .

976
00:56:56,539 --> 00:56:58,624
Forty million dollars .

977
00:57:00,042 --> 00:57:03,170
But , obviously , what we spent on the sets ,

978
00:57:03,337 --> 00:57:04,714
on the sumptuous sets ,

979
00:57:04,880 --> 00:57:08,384
we had to make up on the shooting time .

980
00:57:08,551 --> 00:57:10,553
That was the compromise ,

981
00:57:10,720 --> 00:57:13,514
and I decided not to save on the visuals ,

982
00:57:13,681 --> 00:57:17,852
and to make it in the time allotted , which wasn 't always easy .

983
00:57:21,564 --> 00:57:25,026
This scene was shot in an extraordinary location ,

984
00:57:25,192 --> 00:57:27,612
which used to be a hotel ,

985
00:57:28,237 --> 00:57:31,115
and was abandoned ...

986
00:57:31,490 --> 00:57:34,368
Abandoned to the rats , to squatters ,

987
00:57:34,535 --> 00:57:35,911
to all sorts of insects ...

988
00:57:36,078 --> 00:57:39,540
It was a truly sumptuous location for our purposes .

989
00:57:39,707 --> 00:57:42,960
It was a piece of Silent Hill we found , but real .

990
00:57:43,127 --> 00:57:45,087
We didn 't add many props .

991
00:57:45,254 --> 00:57:48,215
It 's the place pretty much as it was .

992
00:57:48,382 --> 00:57:50,384
This caused a problem

993
00:57:50,551 --> 00:57:53,929
because the dust that was in this ...

994
00:57:54,638 --> 00:57:56,182
in this location

995
00:57:56,349 --> 00:57:57,600
was quite toxic .

996
00:57:57,767 --> 00:58:00,895
We had it analysed and it contained spores ...

997
00:58:02,855 --> 00:58:05,691
of a sort of mould or fungus ,

998
00:58:05,858 --> 00:58:10,613
which could cause serious allergic attacks if breathed in .

999
00:58:10,780 --> 00:58:14,241
There were also pigeon droppings ...

1000
00:58:14,408 --> 00:58:16,994
The building was full of pigeons .

1001
00:58:17,161 --> 00:58:18,329
Rats and so on .

1002
00:58:18,496 --> 00:58:22,166
All of this together made quite an explosive mixture .

1003
00:58:22,333 --> 00:58:25,836
So , in order to be able to shoot in this building ,

1004
00:58:26,003 --> 00:58:28,005
we had to spray a sort of glue ,

1005
00:58:28,172 --> 00:58:30,841
which kept the dust stuck to the ground .

1006
00:58:31,008 --> 00:58:34,303
Because , of course , the technicians ' union

1007
00:58:34,470 --> 00:58:39,016
said we couldn 't work in such an unhealthy place ,

1008
00:58:39,183 --> 00:58:40,976
so we reached

1009
00:58:41,143 --> 00:58:44,021
a sort of compromise ,

1010
00:58:44,188 --> 00:58:46,065
by glueing the dust ,

1011
00:58:46,232 --> 00:58:51,445
spraying this thin skin of glue over everything ,

1012
00:58:51,612 --> 00:58:55,741
so that we could film in there without kicking up the dust .

1013
00:58:56,784 --> 00:58:58,244
That 's how we managed it .

1014
00:58:58,411 --> 00:59:02,957
For a while it looked unlikely because of those health concerns .

1015
00:59:07,461 --> 00:59:11,966
The scenes with Sean Bean were deliberately shot like a film noir .

1016
00:59:12,925 --> 00:59:14,802
That was intentional .

1017
00:59:15,761 --> 00:59:19,974
The atmosphere you get in American movies of the 1940s .

1018
00:59:21,058 --> 00:59:24,603
The bricks , the rain , the empty buildings ,

1019
00:59:24,770 --> 00:59:27,231
the wet trenchcoat and so on ...

1020
00:59:27,398 --> 00:59:32,403
I thought Sean Bean 's face really looked the part .

1021
00:59:32,570 --> 00:59:36,490
Sometimes it takes a lot of effort to create a film noir feeling ,

1022
00:59:36,657 --> 00:59:39,702
with lots of props ... But I think it 's interesting

1023
00:59:39,869 --> 00:59:43,622
that film noir , a major genre in the history of cinema ,

1024
00:59:43,789 --> 00:59:46,250
but a risky one to reference ,

1025
00:59:46,417 --> 00:59:49,128
can be evoked by just a face .

1026
00:59:49,295 --> 00:59:54,175
And I think Sean Bean 's face lends itself to this transcription .

1027
00:59:54,341 --> 00:59:56,260
It has that film noir essence .

1028
00:59:58,304 --> 01:00:00,765
I tried something risky here ,

1029
01:00:00,931 --> 01:00:04,935
by giving the film two levels of understanding .

1030
01:00:05,102 --> 01:00:08,355
There 's what Radha Mitchell comes to understand ,

1031
01:00:08,522 --> 01:00:10,065
investigating inside Silent Hill ,

1032
01:00:10,232 --> 01:00:12,651
and there 's what the father works out ,

1033
01:00:12,818 --> 01:00:15,362
investigating outside Silent Hill .

1034
01:00:15,529 --> 01:00:19,700
I wanted to see how the two interpretations could come together .

1035
01:00:19,867 --> 01:00:22,870
This is sort of what I tried to do

1036
01:00:23,037 --> 01:00:26,916
when I was asked to add scenes with Sean Bean :

1037
01:00:28,125 --> 01:00:30,419
to suggest several interpretations .

1038
01:00:30,586 --> 01:00:33,506
This was quite difficult to do .

1039
01:00:33,672 --> 01:00:34,798
In fact ,

1040
01:00:34,965 --> 01:00:39,887
as the film is already giving the audience plenty to interpret ,

1041
01:00:40,054 --> 01:00:43,724
maybe it was quite a risky thing to attempt .

1042
01:00:46,435 --> 01:00:48,521
But I was swept along

1043
01:00:48,687 --> 01:00:50,856
by the idea that this aspect ...

1044
01:00:51,023 --> 01:00:53,317
People are used to this aspect ,

1045
01:00:53,484 --> 01:00:57,238
because it 's something you find in series like The X-Files ,

1046
01:00:57,404 --> 01:01:00,783
or , more recently than that , in Lost .

1047
01:01:00,950 --> 01:01:04,995
I mean that each character represents one viewpoint ,

1048
01:01:05,162 --> 01:01:07,873
one interpretation , about what 's going on .

1049
01:01:08,040 --> 01:01:11,961
You don 't give a direct explanation of the supernatural ,

1050
01:01:12,127 --> 01:01:14,588
or of these incredible events ,

1051
01:01:14,755 --> 01:01:18,092
but there are as many interpretations as there are viewpoints

1052
01:01:18,259 --> 01:01:19,885
represented by the characters .

1053
01:01:20,052 --> 01:01:24,598
And this encouraged me in thinking that it could work .

1054
01:01:24,765 --> 01:01:28,894
It 's what made The X-Files and Lost so popular .

1055
01:01:35,818 --> 01:01:40,030
Here 's another illustration of what I call the labyrinth theme

1056
01:01:40,197 --> 01:01:42,199
within Silent Hill ,

1057
01:01:42,366 --> 01:01:45,369
the idea that each decor hides another ,

1058
01:01:45,536 --> 01:01:49,039
that each decor is in fact concealing another decor ,

1059
01:01:49,206 --> 01:01:53,127
and that the more keys you find to go through these layers ,

1060
01:01:53,294 --> 01:01:56,088
the more you descend inside yourself .

1061
01:01:56,922 --> 01:01:58,090
This is something

1062
01:01:58,882 --> 01:02:02,136
that 's really fascinating about how video games work ,

1063
01:02:02,303 --> 01:02:06,223
the idea that the labyrinth doesn 't only represent

1064
01:02:06,390 --> 01:02:08,809
the game 's arithmetic architecture ,

1065
01:02:08,976 --> 01:02:11,812
but also the player 's psyche .

1066
01:02:19,361 --> 01:02:22,573
A film that puts across this labyrinth idea really well ,

1067
01:02:22,740 --> 01:02:26,076
this idea of a place where everything 's about codes ,

1068
01:02:26,243 --> 01:02:29,204
about signs and numbers to be deciphered ,

1069
01:02:29,371 --> 01:02:33,584
is a film that 's become more and more famous with time ,

1070
01:02:33,751 --> 01:02:36,879
Dario Argento 's Suspiria ,

1071
01:02:37,671 --> 01:02:41,008
which established this sort of arithmetic architecture ,

1072
01:02:41,175 --> 01:02:44,219
using the environment of a dance academy ,

1073
01:02:44,386 --> 01:02:45,929
and did so

1074
01:02:46,096 --> 01:02:50,184
by giving this absolutely monstrous dimension to the sets .

1075
01:02:52,770 --> 01:02:56,440
I 've never hidden the fact that Dario Argento

1076
01:02:56,607 --> 01:02:58,734
was a big influence on me ,

1077
01:02:58,901 --> 01:03:01,278
not just in making me want to make films ,

1078
01:03:01,445 --> 01:03:05,115
but also in defining my own style .

1079
01:03:05,282 --> 01:03:08,827
That way in which he always represented

1080
01:03:08,994 --> 01:03:11,997
the " beauty " of horror , if I can put it like that ,

1081
01:03:12,164 --> 01:03:16,418
realising that horror isn 't just a situation , but an atmosphere ,

1082
01:03:16,585 --> 01:03:19,296
sometimes a whole world folding over you ,

1083
01:03:19,463 --> 01:03:22,341
and with him this was extremely sensual .

1084
01:03:22,508 --> 01:03:25,302
His main characters are often heroines ,

1085
01:03:25,469 --> 01:03:28,472
pursuing occult secrets ,

1086
01:03:28,639 --> 01:03:33,894
secrets often related to witches or evil influences in this world ,

1087
01:03:34,687 --> 01:03:39,650
where the demonology refers directly to politics or sociology .

1088
01:03:39,817 --> 01:03:43,153
All of this means that there 's a link ,

1089
01:03:43,320 --> 01:03:44,989
by no means a hidden one ,

1090
01:03:45,155 --> 01:03:49,868
between Silent Hill and Dario Argento .

1091
01:03:50,744 --> 01:03:54,456
But I think that to some extent the people who made Silent Hill

1092
01:03:54,623 --> 01:03:58,335
are fans of films like Suspiria .

1093
01:03:58,502 --> 01:04:02,214
All of that , I think , gets mixed up

1094
01:04:02,381 --> 01:04:06,385
in the way they conceive of demonology ,

1095
01:04:06,552 --> 01:04:09,680
as it is understood by folk traditions of the West ,

1096
01:04:09,847 --> 01:04:13,892
and which was very well illustrated by Dario .

1097
01:04:18,188 --> 01:04:23,569
The incinerated sacrificial chamber

1098
01:04:24,445 --> 01:04:27,364
was the worst set we had to shoot in on this film .

1099
01:04:27,531 --> 01:04:31,368
All the burnt bits of the set were real burnt planks ,

1100
01:04:31,535 --> 01:04:35,289
taken from burnt buildings and so on .

1101
01:04:35,456 --> 01:04:38,125
It was very hot . We were under big windows ,

1102
01:04:38,292 --> 01:04:41,378
which you see in one of the low-angle shots .

1103
01:04:41,545 --> 01:04:45,758
As well as that , the fumes from the burnt wood , the charcoal ,

1104
01:04:45,924 --> 01:04:48,218
made the atmosphere unbearable .

1105
01:04:48,385 --> 01:04:51,138
The shooting was very unpleasant .

1106
01:04:51,305 --> 01:04:55,392
We also had the nasty surprise that one of the reels was scratched ,

1107
01:04:55,559 --> 01:04:57,644
so we had to shoot again .

1108
01:04:57,811 --> 01:05:01,690
It was the place we filmed in which most made me

1109
01:05:01,857 --> 01:05:04,610
want to get the hell out of there !

1110
01:05:07,029 --> 01:05:10,699
This is one of the most interesting things about this scene ,

1111
01:05:10,866 --> 01:05:14,578
that the character has to jump , like in a platform game ,

1112
01:05:14,745 --> 01:05:17,998
from one piece of metal to another , above an abyss .

1113
01:05:18,165 --> 01:05:22,836
And Radha Mitchell insisted on doing her own stunts in this scene .

1114
01:05:23,003 --> 01:05:27,007
Obviously , she had a safety cable on ,

1115
01:05:27,174 --> 01:05:30,093
like circus acrobats , but she did the stunts ,

1116
01:05:30,260 --> 01:05:34,139
jumping from one metal beam to the next .

1117
01:05:34,807 --> 01:05:37,935
This just shows that her approach to acting

1118
01:05:38,102 --> 01:05:39,812
has nothing abstract about it .

1119
01:05:39,978 --> 01:05:43,816
She likes playing real-life characters

1120
01:05:43,982 --> 01:05:46,360
confronted with real problems .

1121
01:05:46,527 --> 01:05:50,239
I think this is why , at first , she wasn 't keen to make this film ,

1122
01:05:50,405 --> 01:05:53,075
which is structurally very abstract .

1123
01:05:53,242 --> 01:05:57,663
I think she 's someone who needs real things around her ,

1124
01:05:57,830 --> 01:05:59,498
who needs to do real things .

1125
01:05:59,665 --> 01:06:02,584
I recently saw a good film with her in , Rogue ,

1126
01:06:02,751 --> 01:06:06,255
by Greg McLean , with a gigantic crocodile .

1127
01:06:06,421 --> 01:06:10,008
She plays a guide in the Australian outback ,

1128
01:06:10,175 --> 01:06:12,136
who faces this monster .

1129
01:06:12,845 --> 01:06:14,930
And she 's great in the film ,

1130
01:06:15,097 --> 01:06:19,810
because she 's acting in situations that are very clearly defined .

1131
01:06:19,977 --> 01:06:24,940
She 's one of these actors who need to attach themselves to something real ,

1132
01:06:25,399 --> 01:06:27,234
in order to function .

1133
01:06:29,153 --> 01:06:31,697
In this scene , Radha Mitchell

1134
01:06:31,864 --> 01:06:35,409
is confronted with an incarnation of Alessa ,

1135
01:06:35,576 --> 01:06:39,413
who takes the appearance of a little " Italian-style " ghost ,

1136
01:06:39,580 --> 01:06:41,790
with this baroque aspect ,

1137
01:06:42,708 --> 01:06:47,713
the rotting uniform , the strange face and so on .

1138
01:06:47,880 --> 01:06:49,256
People say ,

1139
01:06:49,423 --> 01:06:52,926
" Oh , that 's a reference to modern Japanese ghost films . "

1140
01:06:53,093 --> 01:06:56,680
What they call J-Horror . But it 's not at all .

1141
01:06:57,639 --> 01:07:00,267
I love J-Horror , and especially Ring ,

1142
01:07:00,434 --> 01:07:02,102
the Japanese original ,

1143
01:07:02,269 --> 01:07:06,315
my favourite horror and fantasy film , along with The Others of Amenábar

1144
01:07:06,481 --> 01:07:08,692
of the last ten years .

1145
01:07:08,859 --> 01:07:10,986
It 's not a reference to J-Horror ,

1146
01:07:11,153 --> 01:07:13,989
it 's a reference to the little ghosts

1147
01:07:14,948 --> 01:07:17,242
in Italian horror and fantasy ,

1148
01:07:17,409 --> 01:07:21,330
especially in Bava 's Kill Baby ... Kill ! , which I mentioned earlier ,

1149
01:07:21,496 --> 01:07:26,585
but also in Fellini 's sketch , Toby Dammit ,

1150
01:07:26,752 --> 01:07:29,379
in Spirits of the Dead ,

1151
01:07:29,546 --> 01:07:31,632
an important film .

1152
01:07:31,924 --> 01:07:35,093
And I particularly liked Fellini 's section in the film .

1153
01:07:35,260 --> 01:07:38,889
It 's more a reference to that , and not at all to J-Horror .

1154
01:07:42,851 --> 01:07:44,478
It 's quite amusing

1155
01:07:44,770 --> 01:07:47,731
that after this , Laurie Holden was in The Mist ,

1156
01:07:47,898 --> 01:07:50,067
a film by Frank Darabont

1157
01:07:50,234 --> 01:07:53,570
all about a mist that envelops a little town ,

1158
01:07:53,737 --> 01:07:55,822
bringing monsters with it .

1159
01:07:57,699 --> 01:07:59,117
It 's quite amusing

1160
01:07:59,284 --> 01:08:03,830
to see how , quite often , actors get typecast

1161
01:08:03,997 --> 01:08:08,418
in films which tackle similar sorts of themes .

1162
01:08:08,585 --> 01:08:12,965
What 's more , Sean Bean made a film called The Dark , in Canada ,

1163
01:08:13,840 --> 01:08:16,426
a film with certain similarities ,

1164
01:08:16,593 --> 01:08:19,596
because it 's about a couple , he and Maria Bello ,

1165
01:08:19,763 --> 01:08:22,140
looking for their daughter .

1166
01:08:22,307 --> 01:08:25,644
So it 's quite interesting to see these actors

1167
01:08:25,811 --> 01:08:28,730
cast , in a way , by different directors ,

1168
01:08:28,897 --> 01:08:33,318
to play roles in worlds where the themes have similarities .

1169
01:08:35,988 --> 01:08:38,740
This amazing church at the top of the stairs

1170
01:08:38,907 --> 01:08:41,201
is a building that really exists .

1171
01:08:41,368 --> 01:08:45,247
It 's a Masonic building , which I found in the suburbs ...

1172
01:08:45,789 --> 01:08:48,875
in the suburbs of a town

1173
01:08:49,042 --> 01:08:51,628
quite close to Toronto .

1174
01:08:51,795 --> 01:08:55,882
This building fascinated me because I really like

1175
01:08:56,300 --> 01:08:59,928
its fortress aspect , its sanctuary aspect ,

1176
01:09:00,095 --> 01:09:04,349
this idea of trying to protect oneself against the outside ,

1177
01:09:04,516 --> 01:09:07,060
which is typical of the architecture

1178
01:09:07,477 --> 01:09:09,229
of this location .

1179
01:09:09,396 --> 01:09:13,942
It interested me to use it . So we filmed the real building

1180
01:09:14,109 --> 01:09:16,903
and constantly incorporated shots of it

1181
01:09:17,070 --> 01:09:21,241
within the film of the studio set ,

1182
01:09:21,408 --> 01:09:24,494
which was just the steps . We only built the steps ,

1183
01:09:24,661 --> 01:09:27,122
and a bit of the entrance .

1184
01:09:27,289 --> 01:09:31,543
The rest isn 't a digital image ,

1185
01:09:31,710 --> 01:09:34,755
it 's a real building , filmed separately ,

1186
01:09:34,921 --> 01:09:36,715
and added to the image .

1187
01:09:38,633 --> 01:09:43,972
It was risky , suddenly introducing all these people into a place

1188
01:09:44,139 --> 01:09:47,559
which had seemed up until that point to be deserted .

1189
01:09:47,976 --> 01:09:51,980
The idea is that this is the last group of inhabitants ,

1190
01:09:52,147 --> 01:09:54,941
and that when the siren sounds ,

1191
01:09:55,108 --> 01:09:57,486
they come and take sanctuary here ,

1192
01:09:57,652 --> 01:09:59,279
which is the only place

1193
01:09:59,446 --> 01:10:02,324
that the creatures released by Alessa can 't enter .

1194
01:10:02,908 --> 01:10:07,954
This was , obviously , a risk .

1195
01:10:08,121 --> 01:10:11,124
I discussed this at length with Akira Yamaoka .

1196
01:10:11,291 --> 01:10:16,713
For him , anything that could develop or enrich the mythology of Silent Hill

1197
01:10:16,880 --> 01:10:21,593
was welcome , as long as it fitted in with the original premise .

1198
01:10:21,760 --> 01:10:24,763
At the time , Akira Yamaoka was looking

1199
01:10:24,930 --> 01:10:28,725
for ideas for the next Silent Hill .

1200
01:10:28,892 --> 01:10:31,353
Silent Hill 4 had been released

1201
01:10:31,520 --> 01:10:33,480
but the fans hadn 't liked it .

1202
01:10:33,647 --> 01:10:36,191
I love Silent Hill 4 : The Room .

1203
01:10:36,358 --> 01:10:38,527
But it wasn 't liked , I think ,

1204
01:10:38,693 --> 01:10:41,905
because it moved too far from the original concept .

1205
01:10:42,197 --> 01:10:47,244
So Akira Yamaoka wanted to get back to the original concept ,

1206
01:10:47,577 --> 01:10:50,831
while enriching it with stuff about the town 's history .

1207
01:10:50,997 --> 01:10:53,834
And everything we were doing in the film

1208
01:10:54,000 --> 01:10:56,294
really interested him .

1209
01:10:56,461 --> 01:10:58,588
He took notes and so on .

1210
01:10:58,880 --> 01:11:02,926
I think that , in a way , it helped fertilise his imagination ,

1211
01:11:03,093 --> 01:11:06,596
and gave him a direction to go in with the next game .

1212
01:11:06,763 --> 01:11:09,766
Nothing came of it in the end because ,

1213
01:11:09,933 --> 01:11:12,352
to my great personal disappointment ...

1214
01:11:14,855 --> 01:11:18,316
the game Silent Hill was taken away from its Japanese creators

1215
01:11:18,483 --> 01:11:20,026
and given to an American team ,

1216
01:11:20,402 --> 01:11:22,988
who made Silent Hill 5 .

1217
01:11:23,155 --> 01:11:25,407
So it 's no longer the Japanese

1218
01:11:25,574 --> 01:11:28,160
making the Silent Hill games .

1219
01:11:28,326 --> 01:11:32,289
Personally , maybe because I 'm a director and scriptwriter ,

1220
01:11:32,456 --> 01:11:34,791
I 'd have preferred it

1221
01:11:34,958 --> 01:11:39,296
if the original team was still involved with future Silent Hill games .

1222
01:11:41,548 --> 01:11:45,093
The inside of the church was totally rebuilt in the studio .

1223
01:11:45,260 --> 01:11:48,680
It was a very impressive set ,

1224
01:11:48,847 --> 01:11:50,223
and a bit intimidating .

1225
01:11:51,141 --> 01:11:52,726
It was going to be bigger !

1226
01:11:52,893 --> 01:11:55,645
But we decided , for budgetary reasons ,

1227
01:11:55,812 --> 01:11:58,648
to make it a bit smaller than planned .

1228
01:11:58,815 --> 01:12:03,361
That was the right course to take because its scale caused me problems .

1229
01:12:03,528 --> 01:12:06,281
Especially during the final scene .

1230
01:12:06,656 --> 01:12:08,700
So it 's a set

1231
01:12:08,867 --> 01:12:12,829
that was very impressive , and ...

1232
01:12:13,121 --> 01:12:15,749
And it shows

1233
01:12:15,916 --> 01:12:20,337
the epic and theatrical aspect of Carol Spier 's talent .

1234
01:12:20,504 --> 01:12:23,006
That was one of the reasons I chose

1235
01:12:23,173 --> 01:12:26,384
this production designer , the fact that there 's

1236
01:12:26,551 --> 01:12:29,679
an epic and theatrical scale to what she does .

1237
01:12:29,846 --> 01:12:33,725
She 's not just a talented set decorator .

1238
01:12:33,892 --> 01:12:38,438
There is always something larger than life

1239
01:12:38,605 --> 01:12:39,814
in her work ,

1240
01:12:39,981 --> 01:12:43,401
something incredibly huge and epic .

1241
01:12:43,568 --> 01:12:47,322
I chose Alice Krige to play the big baddy , Christabella ,

1242
01:12:47,489 --> 01:12:49,282
because I love her as an actress .

1243
01:12:49,449 --> 01:12:52,369
I particularly like her in the films

1244
01:12:52,536 --> 01:12:53,828
of the Quay brothers .

1245
01:12:53,995 --> 01:12:58,250
She made a film I really liked called Institute Benjamenta .

1246
01:12:59,209 --> 01:13:00,418
She was amazing .

1247
01:13:00,585 --> 01:13:04,381
And I remembered her , of course , as the ghost in Ghost Story ,

1248
01:13:04,965 --> 01:13:07,509
which was quite an interesting film .

1249
01:13:07,676 --> 01:13:09,594
She 's an actress

1250
01:13:10,136 --> 01:13:13,974
with a great reputation . She 's very professional .

1251
01:13:14,432 --> 01:13:18,603
She does a lot of theatre , and she was just perfect

1252
01:13:18,770 --> 01:13:20,230
to play Christabella ,

1253
01:13:20,397 --> 01:13:22,107
because she 's so imposing .

1254
01:13:22,274 --> 01:13:25,986
She imposed her character on all the other actors

1255
01:13:26,152 --> 01:13:28,989
that we chose to play the members of her sect .

1256
01:13:29,155 --> 01:13:31,533
A very imposing manner .

1257
01:13:31,700 --> 01:13:33,952
She was admired on the set ,

1258
01:13:34,119 --> 01:13:37,872
both by the crew and by those acting around her .

1259
01:13:38,123 --> 01:13:42,085
This sequence , passing from the sect praying in the church ,

1260
01:13:42,252 --> 01:13:43,920
to the children praying ,

1261
01:13:44,087 --> 01:13:48,341
was a way for me to defuse the tension of the film ,

1262
01:13:48,508 --> 01:13:51,428
showing what I think is bad religion ,

1263
01:13:51,594 --> 01:13:55,390
and what is " good " religion , practised in a decent way .

1264
01:13:55,557 --> 01:13:59,811
So you get the intolerance of the sect ,

1265
01:13:59,978 --> 01:14:04,316
juxtaposed with the children praying , which for me

1266
01:14:04,482 --> 01:14:07,110
is a comforting vision of religion .

1267
01:14:07,652 --> 01:14:10,155
Seeing these scenes reminds me

1268
01:14:11,406 --> 01:14:16,369
that part of the style of Silent Hill , of this film ,

1269
01:14:16,536 --> 01:14:20,165
I discovered , or elaborated ,

1270
01:14:20,332 --> 01:14:23,418
while I was co-producing a film

1271
01:14:23,585 --> 01:14:25,920
called House of Voices , by Pascal Laugier ,

1272
01:14:26,087 --> 01:14:29,883
which is a film with lots of similar themes .

1273
01:14:30,050 --> 01:14:33,219
It was really interesting , watching Pascal directing .

1274
01:14:33,386 --> 01:14:37,098
He 's an immensely talented young director .

1275
01:14:38,641 --> 01:14:43,313
So I watched him directing and working with this sort of imagery

1276
01:14:43,480 --> 01:14:48,568
at the time I was working on ... or when Silent Hill was on my mind ,

1277
01:14:48,735 --> 01:14:52,947
and I think there 's a sort of dialogue between the two films .

1278
01:14:53,114 --> 01:14:55,950
There 's something similar about the way

1279
01:14:56,117 --> 01:14:59,371
in which the religious environment is depicted ,

1280
01:15:00,121 --> 01:15:02,457
in the interests of a ghost story ,

1281
01:15:02,624 --> 01:15:06,836
a ghost story originating in a character 's mind .

1282
01:15:07,837 --> 01:15:11,841
So there are plenty of points in common ,

1283
01:15:12,008 --> 01:15:15,053
as there are with his new film , Martyrs .

1284
01:15:15,220 --> 01:15:19,265
For example , there 's the image of the woman in the cellar ,

1285
01:15:19,432 --> 01:15:21,476
tortured with the steel helmet ,

1286
01:15:21,643 --> 01:15:25,021
which could bring to mind the image

1287
01:15:25,188 --> 01:15:28,441
of the janitor in the Silent Hill school .

1288
01:15:28,608 --> 01:15:32,028
The two of us are very close , we 're good friends ,

1289
01:15:32,195 --> 01:15:36,282
we both talk a lot about the cinema of the fantastic ,

1290
01:15:36,950 --> 01:15:40,662
and besides producing his film , what mainly interested me

1291
01:15:40,829 --> 01:15:43,206
was helping him get started

1292
01:15:43,373 --> 01:15:46,251
because I was convinced of his talent .

1293
01:15:46,751 --> 01:15:49,546
And I think that , without his knowing it ,

1294
01:15:49,712 --> 01:15:54,801
the way he made House of Voices had a big influence on me and Silent Hill .

1295
01:16:02,058 --> 01:16:04,436
The paradox with the church scenes

1296
01:16:04,602 --> 01:16:08,022
was that the set was huge , absolutely enormous ,

1297
01:16:08,189 --> 01:16:12,110
but the scenes had to be shot , paradoxically , very quickly .

1298
01:16:14,779 --> 01:16:20,577
All the scenes in the church took very little time , in fact .

1299
01:16:20,743 --> 01:16:24,581
I think that , in all , they took four or five days .

1300
01:16:25,915 --> 01:16:30,754
Only a day and a half was put by for the final scene in the church .

1301
01:16:31,504 --> 01:16:36,426
That was mostly due to the work schedule .

1302
01:16:36,593 --> 01:16:39,846
All the church scenes were left until the end ,

1303
01:16:40,013 --> 01:16:45,393
so whenever any cutting back was done , it cut into the big final scenes .

1304
01:16:45,894 --> 01:16:48,354
It was a bit of a gamble on my part , thinking ,

1305
01:16:48,521 --> 01:16:54,319
" If I leave the big scenes till last , they won 't dare stop me . "

1306
01:16:54,736 --> 01:16:57,530
But the problem , in fact ,

1307
01:16:57,697 --> 01:17:02,911
was that the insurance on the film ,

1308
01:17:03,077 --> 01:17:04,954
the completion bond ,

1309
01:17:05,121 --> 01:17:10,251
meant that people were sent to make sure it finished on time , in 61 days .

1310
01:17:10,418 --> 01:17:12,003
And their arrival

1311
01:17:12,170 --> 01:17:16,341
forced us to film the church scenes very quickly ,

1312
01:17:16,508 --> 01:17:18,718
and even changing the end .

1313
01:17:18,885 --> 01:17:22,680
The end we 'd planned wasn 't what we finally filmed .

1314
01:17:24,015 --> 01:17:27,143
That 's all part of filmmaking nowadays ,

1315
01:17:27,310 --> 01:17:30,688
the fact that the ...

1316
01:17:31,564 --> 01:17:34,234
the insurance agents have all the power ,

1317
01:17:34,400 --> 01:17:38,238
if ever you happen to overrun your schedule .

1318
01:17:38,404 --> 01:17:41,199
They already had me in their sights , anyway ,

1319
01:17:41,366 --> 01:17:45,620
because they 'd heard of how Brotherhood of the Wolf had overrun ,

1320
01:17:45,787 --> 01:17:48,957
mostly because of weather problems

1321
01:17:50,333 --> 01:17:51,960
and outside shooting ,

1322
01:17:52,126 --> 01:17:55,463
but as far as they were concerned

1323
01:17:55,630 --> 01:17:59,133
I was a somewhat " dangerous " director .

1324
01:17:59,884 --> 01:18:04,556
So they paid particular attention to the way I filmed Silent Hill .

1325
01:18:04,722 --> 01:18:08,977
In a way , they forced me to make the film on schedule ,

1326
01:18:09,143 --> 01:18:12,689
which was good , really , because it was so complicated to make

1327
01:18:12,855 --> 01:18:15,900
that I couldn 't have endured another day on the set .

1328
01:18:16,067 --> 01:18:20,488
In any case , the completion bond people went home ,

1329
01:18:20,655 --> 01:18:24,951
totally reassured about my ability to finish a film on schedule .

1330
01:18:36,880 --> 01:18:41,301
That phrase , " A mother is God in the eyes of her child , "

1331
01:18:41,718 --> 01:18:45,722
is , if I can put it like this , the cure for the sickness ,

1332
01:18:45,888 --> 01:18:46,973
in this film .

1333
01:18:47,140 --> 01:18:49,267
As far as I 'm concerned ,

1334
01:18:49,434 --> 01:18:52,979
you can 't make a noir film without giving some hint

1335
01:18:53,146 --> 01:18:56,649
of where there might be light , where the end of the tunnel is .

1336
01:18:56,816 --> 01:18:59,277
And that phrase , for me ,

1337
01:18:59,444 --> 01:19:00,361
is that hint .

1338
01:19:00,528 --> 01:19:05,241
It effectively demonstrates my conception of " good "

1339
01:19:05,491 --> 01:19:06,451
in this film ,

1340
01:19:06,618 --> 01:19:10,914
which is otherwise very dark , gloomy and depressing , perhaps .

1341
01:19:11,706 --> 01:19:13,916
What I was trying to say was ,

1342
01:19:14,083 --> 01:19:18,046
" If there 's a cure for all this despair , then that is it .

1343
01:19:18,212 --> 01:19:20,506
" That 's how you should interpret the film . "

1344
01:19:20,673 --> 01:19:25,762
I 've always thought that films which are totally dark ,

1345
01:19:25,929 --> 01:19:27,972
with no spark of light ,

1346
01:19:28,139 --> 01:19:30,642
are films which , even if excellent ,

1347
01:19:30,808 --> 01:19:34,479
are in some way condemned to not being understood ,

1348
01:19:35,229 --> 01:19:37,857
condemned to remain in obscurity ,

1349
01:19:38,024 --> 01:19:41,194
in the obscurity which they themselves created .

1350
01:19:41,361 --> 01:19:44,864
It 's always risky not to give people a way out .

1351
01:19:46,282 --> 01:19:49,786
When you 're a director - this is how I see it -

1352
01:19:49,952 --> 01:19:53,414
one of your responsibilities

1353
01:19:53,581 --> 01:19:55,750
is to show where the exit is

1354
01:19:55,917 --> 01:20:00,254
from a world that is so radically unpleasant .

1355
01:20:03,174 --> 01:20:07,512
This map is directly inspired by the maps in the game Silent Hill .

1356
01:20:07,679 --> 01:20:10,932
They look like this , and when you 're lost

1357
01:20:11,099 --> 01:20:15,645
you can get your bearings , more or less , by following ...

1358
01:20:15,812 --> 01:20:18,815
by going and looking at these maps .

1359
01:20:18,981 --> 01:20:22,777
So it amused me to reproduce them in a hospital ,

1360
01:20:22,944 --> 01:20:26,531
where you do get such guides to help you find your way

1361
01:20:26,698 --> 01:20:31,035
through the maze of huge administrative buildings .

1362
01:20:32,578 --> 01:20:35,498
When Dan Laustsen and I did the screen tests ,

1363
01:20:35,665 --> 01:20:40,086
we realised that there were distinct ways of filming Radha Mitchell

1364
01:20:40,253 --> 01:20:41,629
and Laurie Holden .

1365
01:20:41,796 --> 01:20:43,423
Radha Mitchell , for example ,

1366
01:20:43,589 --> 01:20:47,802
needed a slightly high-angle shot with a short lens ,

1367
01:20:48,469 --> 01:20:51,556
whereas it was better to shoot Laurie Holden

1368
01:20:51,723 --> 01:20:55,184
from a low-angle shot , with a long focal length .

1369
01:20:56,227 --> 01:21:00,314
Their two faces ' structures were so distinct

1370
01:21:00,481 --> 01:21:04,318
that we had to keep changing lens to shoot one or the other .

1371
01:21:04,485 --> 01:21:06,654
And this scene is typical

1372
01:21:07,447 --> 01:21:10,658
of the way we worked on their faces .

1373
01:21:10,825 --> 01:21:16,080
So I used a bit of a Kubrick style to shoot Radha Mitchell ,

1374
01:21:16,706 --> 01:21:20,710
always this slightly high-angle shot to bring out her forehead ,

1375
01:21:20,877 --> 01:21:23,212
whereas with Laurie Holden

1376
01:21:23,379 --> 01:21:28,217
we 're always at a low angle , with her face raised .

1377
01:21:28,968 --> 01:21:32,263
This fight scene with Laurie Holden ...

1378
01:21:32,430 --> 01:21:34,807
As I said before , she 's an actress

1379
01:21:34,974 --> 01:21:38,686
who gives it everything she 's got , right from take one .

1380
01:21:38,853 --> 01:21:41,731
In this scene she really does fight .

1381
01:21:41,898 --> 01:21:43,983
She really does land those blows !

1382
01:21:45,193 --> 01:21:47,862
Those guys have iron helmets ,

1383
01:21:48,029 --> 01:21:51,115
and she really is hitting them .

1384
01:21:51,282 --> 01:21:54,744
When you see a guy go flying , it 's because she whacked him .

1385
01:21:54,911 --> 01:21:58,748
So much so , in fact , that she had to go to hospital

1386
01:21:58,915 --> 01:22:01,083
because she 'd badly hurt her hand .

1387
01:22:01,250 --> 01:22:04,003
And when we came to finish the scene ,

1388
01:22:04,962 --> 01:22:07,757
we had to be careful she didn 't do it again .

1389
01:22:07,924 --> 01:22:10,426
That 's typical of her , going all out .

1390
01:22:12,720 --> 01:22:14,931
It isn 't an action film .

1391
01:22:15,097 --> 01:22:19,143
It 's the only film I 've made with hardly any action scenes .

1392
01:22:20,603 --> 01:22:22,897
When I start a new project ,

1393
01:22:23,064 --> 01:22:25,817
I try to make it anti what I did before .

1394
01:22:25,983 --> 01:22:29,320
So Brotherhood of the Wolf was anti Crying Freeman ,

1395
01:22:29,487 --> 01:22:32,740
and Silent Hill was anti Brotherhood of the Wolf .

1396
01:22:32,907 --> 01:22:35,076
I try to muddy the waters .

1397
01:22:35,243 --> 01:22:37,745
People expect this , so I 'll do that .

1398
01:22:37,912 --> 01:22:43,209
People expect slo-mo , action scenes , kung-fu and so on .

1399
01:22:43,793 --> 01:22:45,711
Well , this 'll be different !

1400
01:22:45,878 --> 01:22:47,129
I always try

1401
01:22:47,296 --> 01:22:49,549
to play with people 's expectations ,

1402
01:22:49,715 --> 01:22:52,051
not just to confound those

1403
01:22:52,218 --> 01:22:56,013
who want to put me in a neatly labelled box .

1404
01:22:56,180 --> 01:23:00,184
I 'm just constantly trying to be a little bit surprising .

1405
01:23:01,352 --> 01:23:05,606
So I deliberately made Silent Hill

1406
01:23:05,773 --> 01:23:08,526
with only one bit of slo-mo in the whole film ,

1407
01:23:08,693 --> 01:23:10,778
and hardly any action scenes ,

1408
01:23:11,237 --> 01:23:16,075
to make people say , " He didn 't do what he usually does ! "

1409
01:23:16,576 --> 01:23:19,829
It 's always a bit too easy , I personally think ,

1410
01:23:19,996 --> 01:23:24,292
to reduce a director to his trademark gimmicks and so on .

1411
01:23:25,126 --> 01:23:28,838
And I think it 's fun for a director to go against that .

1412
01:23:30,256 --> 01:23:35,303
This scene of the beating was shot by my 2nd-unit director , Bill Gereghty ,

1413
01:23:35,887 --> 01:23:41,017
who 'd worked with me on Crying Freeman and Brotherhood of the Wolf .

1414
01:23:41,183 --> 01:23:44,896
Bill used to be an assistant to Sam Peckinpah ,

1415
01:23:45,062 --> 01:23:49,150
and when he had to do this scene he asked me , " How do you want it ? "

1416
01:23:49,734 --> 01:23:52,904
And I said , " Like in a Western , of course ! "

1417
01:23:53,070 --> 01:23:55,406
I think when he shot the scene ,

1418
01:23:55,573 --> 01:23:59,619
he must have had in mind Clint Eastwood in A Fistful of Dollars .

1419
01:23:59,785 --> 01:24:04,665
The way he shot Laurie Holden getting beaten up must come from that .

1420
01:24:06,667 --> 01:24:08,169
The Nurses ...

1421
01:24:08,336 --> 01:24:11,964
There are swarms of these creatures in Silent Hill ,

1422
01:24:12,465 --> 01:24:15,092
and they 're very specific to the game .

1423
01:24:15,259 --> 01:24:17,970
The fans literally adore them ,

1424
01:24:18,137 --> 01:24:19,639
and I understand why ,

1425
01:24:19,805 --> 01:24:22,600
because they 're a combination of horror

1426
01:24:22,767 --> 01:24:25,394
and an extremely sexy attitude ,

1427
01:24:25,561 --> 01:24:28,814
an eroticism that is in no way concealed .

1428
01:24:28,981 --> 01:24:31,150
Doing the Nurses was complicated ,

1429
01:24:31,317 --> 01:24:35,196
precisely because they are such emblematic creatures ,

1430
01:24:35,363 --> 01:24:38,699
and because they move around in gangs .

1431
01:24:38,866 --> 01:24:43,579
So we had to organise an enormous casting call

1432
01:24:44,038 --> 01:24:45,164
of lap dancers ,

1433
01:24:45,331 --> 01:24:48,209
because they had the best combination

1434
01:24:48,376 --> 01:24:52,088
of the requisite physical and aesthetic qualities .

1435
01:24:52,255 --> 01:24:54,048
The choreographer

1436
01:24:54,215 --> 01:24:57,426
was very excited about doing a chorus line ,

1437
01:24:57,593 --> 01:25:01,555
with all the dancers lined up like that , in the film .

1438
01:25:01,722 --> 01:25:06,519
And what started out as quite a weird choreography

1439
01:25:06,686 --> 01:25:10,064
practically became a mechanical ballet .

1440
01:25:10,231 --> 01:25:12,733
There was a risk there ,

1441
01:25:12,900 --> 01:25:16,529
since they were going to dance rather than move .

1442
01:25:17,196 --> 01:25:20,157
Would it look like Michael Jackson 's Thriller ?

1443
01:25:20,324 --> 01:25:23,202
That was my fear when we were shooting .

1444
01:25:23,536 --> 01:25:25,371
It is a bit like that .

1445
01:25:25,538 --> 01:25:28,833
In any case , it 's a very strange sequence .

1446
01:25:29,000 --> 01:25:33,879
I wanted to make it closer in mood to the ballet Ophelia ,

1447
01:25:34,046 --> 01:25:36,382
and The Tales of Hoffmann ,

1448
01:25:36,549 --> 01:25:40,344
which are full of characters like clockwork dolls ,

1449
01:25:40,511 --> 01:25:42,388
with jerky movements and so on .

1450
01:25:42,555 --> 01:25:45,266
That 's how I wanted to approach the scene .

1451
01:25:45,433 --> 01:25:47,768
The technique I came up with

1452
01:25:47,935 --> 01:25:50,062
to create Silent Hill 's monsters

1453
01:25:50,229 --> 01:25:52,565
was a combination of ...

1454
01:25:53,482 --> 01:25:54,817
sculpture ,

1455
01:25:54,984 --> 01:25:58,529
meaning a full costume sculpted by Patrick Tatopoulos ,

1456
01:25:59,488 --> 01:26:01,907
a dancer inside it ,

1457
01:26:02,074 --> 01:26:04,201
and special effects

1458
01:26:04,368 --> 01:26:09,165
to create ruptures within the physical rhythms .

1459
01:26:09,540 --> 01:26:14,128
We made it so that there 's a variation in the speeds ,

1460
01:26:14,295 --> 01:26:18,132
to constantly create a kind of fragmented movement ,

1461
01:26:18,299 --> 01:26:21,427
to create something that 's both harmonious

1462
01:26:21,594 --> 01:26:25,014
but also strange and even unpleasant to watch .

1463
01:26:25,598 --> 01:26:28,934
Another essential part of the monsters is the sound .

1464
01:26:29,435 --> 01:26:32,855
This was worked on , with his usual care ,

1465
01:26:33,022 --> 01:26:36,484
by Nicolas Becker , a great French sound designer .

1466
01:26:36,650 --> 01:26:38,778
He did Brotherhood of the Wolf ,

1467
01:26:38,944 --> 01:26:43,074
but also a magnificent recent sci-fi film called Sunshine .

1468
01:26:43,908 --> 01:26:46,494
He 's a true artist of sound .

1469
01:26:47,787 --> 01:26:50,956
His particularity is that he works with natural sounds .

1470
01:26:51,123 --> 01:26:54,335
They often end up sounding very strange ,

1471
01:26:54,502 --> 01:26:58,047
but they 're not synthetic , not created on a synthesiser ,

1472
01:26:58,214 --> 01:27:00,049
they come from real sounds .

1473
01:27:00,800 --> 01:27:03,511
He goes and records sounds in factories ,

1474
01:27:04,720 --> 01:27:06,597
at huge reservoirs ...

1475
01:27:06,764 --> 01:27:11,769
He records the deaf and dumb , he records people and ...

1476
01:27:12,228 --> 01:27:14,939
He gets specific sounds but doesn 't doctor them .

1477
01:27:15,356 --> 01:27:19,360
That 's something about him which I really admire .

1478
01:27:19,735 --> 01:27:21,570
It 's also one reason

1479
01:27:21,737 --> 01:27:24,865
why Silent Hill 's monsters have this organic feel .

1480
01:27:25,032 --> 01:27:28,202
The sound isn 't doctored , it isn 't artificial ,

1481
01:27:28,369 --> 01:27:31,580
it 's a sound which is , in a way , surrealist ,

1482
01:27:31,747 --> 01:27:36,043
which was removed from somewhere else and stuck here .

1483
01:27:36,210 --> 01:27:38,254
It 's out of place .

1484
01:27:38,546 --> 01:27:41,173
Working with him is always a great pleasure .

1485
01:27:42,675 --> 01:27:45,553
Here , the character is faced with the truth ,

1486
01:27:45,719 --> 01:27:48,431
in the form of a blinding light

1487
01:27:48,597 --> 01:27:50,724
and a voice telling her she 's won .

1488
01:27:50,891 --> 01:27:56,105
So that 's another direct reference to the way video games work .

1489
01:27:57,189 --> 01:28:00,025
I think this scene serves a similar purpose

1490
01:28:00,192 --> 01:28:03,320
to the one in The Matrix ,

1491
01:28:03,487 --> 01:28:06,323
where Neo meets the architect .

1492
01:28:06,490 --> 01:28:08,617
What I mean is that

1493
01:28:08,784 --> 01:28:11,996
it 's the moment when the character meets her creator ,

1494
01:28:12,163 --> 01:28:14,331
in the spirit of Pirandello : an endless reflection .

1495
01:28:14,498 --> 01:28:16,917
It 's the idea that , at a certain point ,

1496
01:28:17,084 --> 01:28:18,711
the fictional character

1497
01:28:18,878 --> 01:28:23,382
meets the person who put her in this often uncomfortable situation .

1498
01:28:23,549 --> 01:28:25,801
This is interesting

1499
01:28:25,968 --> 01:28:30,264
because my film has quite a few things in common with The Matrix .

1500
01:28:30,890 --> 01:28:34,727
Both films were produced by Andrew Mason ,

1501
01:28:35,144 --> 01:28:38,147
who was a producer on The Matrix ,

1502
01:28:38,314 --> 01:28:41,066
but also on Dark City and The Crow ,

1503
01:28:41,233 --> 01:28:44,570
which are films that work in a similar way to Silent Hill .

1504
01:28:44,737 --> 01:28:47,448
I 'd almost call them " brain " films ,

1505
01:28:47,615 --> 01:28:52,119
films where the world is created in the imagination of a character ,

1506
01:28:52,286 --> 01:28:54,413
whom you finally meet .

1507
01:28:56,123 --> 01:28:59,752
The flashback sequence wasn 't intended to be like this .

1508
01:28:59,919 --> 01:29:05,466
It was going to look like a 20th Century Fox film from 1960 ,

1509
01:29:05,633 --> 01:29:07,176
produced by Darryl Zanuck .

1510
01:29:07,343 --> 01:29:11,972
I 'd planned to have saturated colours , to shoot it in Cinemascope ,

1511
01:29:12,139 --> 01:29:15,226
with that excessively stylised quality

1512
01:29:15,392 --> 01:29:18,145
that you got in 1960s Fox melodramas ,

1513
01:29:18,312 --> 01:29:19,355
which I love .

1514
01:29:19,521 --> 01:29:21,023
But it turned out

1515
01:29:21,357 --> 01:29:25,694
that to do it like that would cost far too much money ,

1516
01:29:25,861 --> 01:29:29,114
there 'd be too many pieces missing and so on .

1517
01:29:29,698 --> 01:29:32,326
My editor Sébastien Prangère and I

1518
01:29:32,493 --> 01:29:36,830
wondered how we could change tack and make this flashback

1519
01:29:36,997 --> 01:29:41,043
without the missing pieces being evident .

1520
01:29:41,210 --> 01:29:44,380
Then I thought that we could do it

1521
01:29:44,547 --> 01:29:47,633
with a treatment like in Depeche Mode videos ,

1522
01:29:47,800 --> 01:29:51,804
doing the exact opposite of what we 'd originally intended .

1523
01:29:51,971 --> 01:29:54,556
Rather than make a big Fox melodrama ,

1524
01:29:54,723 --> 01:29:59,853
we 'd do it like something shot in Super 8 by Anton Corbjin ,

1525
01:30:00,062 --> 01:30:02,648
something very grainy ,

1526
01:30:02,815 --> 01:30:06,819
like an old home movie with bits missing .

1527
01:30:06,986 --> 01:30:11,615
It was someone who 'd worked with me on the menus of the HK collection

1528
01:30:11,782 --> 01:30:14,410
- the collection of Asian films that I edit -

1529
01:30:14,577 --> 01:30:17,746
who totally created the look of this flashback .

1530
01:30:17,913 --> 01:30:21,500
His name is Kook Ewo , and he still works with me .

1531
01:30:22,418 --> 01:30:25,337
The concept was to go back through all the places

1532
01:30:25,504 --> 01:30:28,507
that we 'd visited in this adventure ,

1533
01:30:28,674 --> 01:30:30,968
and to give an explanation

1534
01:30:31,135 --> 01:30:35,139
why these places were so important to Alessa ,

1535
01:30:35,306 --> 01:30:40,185
how these places had been the theatre of her endless suffering ,

1536
01:30:40,477 --> 01:30:44,064
the pain of a little girl unluckily born to an adulteress ,

1537
01:30:44,231 --> 01:30:45,941
in a sect .

1538
01:30:47,026 --> 01:30:50,321
And who therefore paid for her mother 's sins .

1539
01:30:50,487 --> 01:30:53,907
The torture scene was obviously hard to imagine

1540
01:30:54,074 --> 01:30:57,411
inasmuch as the torture of a child

1541
01:30:57,578 --> 01:31:00,956
is something that today is totally taboo .

1542
01:31:01,123 --> 01:31:02,666
It 's a real taboo .

1543
01:31:02,833 --> 01:31:07,129
So we wondered for a long time how we 'd portray that .

1544
01:31:07,296 --> 01:31:10,716
Originally , the scene was much more explicit .

1545
01:31:10,883 --> 01:31:13,052
But I think it was a good decision

1546
01:31:13,218 --> 01:31:16,930
to leave part of it to the viewer 's imagination .

1547
01:31:20,184 --> 01:31:24,313
In the game it 's obvious that Alessa was tortured

1548
01:31:24,480 --> 01:31:27,399
by the inhabitants of Silent Hill ,

1549
01:31:27,566 --> 01:31:31,862
during some sort of diabolical ritual .

1550
01:31:33,238 --> 01:31:35,991
So there 's a sort of reversal of the idea

1551
01:31:36,158 --> 01:31:41,622
of Alessa getting tortured by a sect of fanatics ,

1552
01:31:41,789 --> 01:31:45,542
so that the sect ends up paying dearly ,

1553
01:31:45,709 --> 01:31:49,546
because Alessa , following her martyrdom , becomes ...

1554
01:31:51,465 --> 01:31:54,009
a spirit , a superior power , an entity ,

1555
01:31:54,176 --> 01:31:57,304
who will degree severe punishment upon them all .

1556
01:32:01,433 --> 01:32:04,686
This scene has a reference to a famous character in the game ,

1557
01:32:05,270 --> 01:32:08,232
a cult character called Lisa Garland ,

1558
01:32:08,399 --> 01:32:11,110
also known as the Red Nurse .

1559
01:32:11,276 --> 01:32:14,697
This was a little nod and wink to the fans ,

1560
01:32:14,863 --> 01:32:18,659
putting Lisa Garland in this scene and explaining why

1561
01:32:18,826 --> 01:32:22,246
she 's stuck in Silent Hill , as she 's just a nurse .

1562
01:32:22,413 --> 01:32:26,250
But she does commit a sin , from Alessa 's point of view ,

1563
01:32:26,417 --> 01:32:29,253
with her unhealthy curiosity ,

1564
01:32:29,420 --> 01:32:31,588
going and peeking at her .

1565
01:32:31,755 --> 01:32:34,341
And this is what damns her .

1566
01:32:38,887 --> 01:32:42,099
This is where , I think , the film gets a bit complicated

1567
01:32:42,266 --> 01:32:47,104
for those who only want to decipher it

1568
01:32:47,271 --> 01:32:50,649
from a Judaeo-Christian conception of good and evil .

1569
01:32:50,816 --> 01:32:55,696
In this scene you can understand that Alessa , in her martyrdom ,

1570
01:32:56,864 --> 01:32:59,616
is visited by the devil ,

1571
01:33:00,534 --> 01:33:02,911
who offers her the chance of vengeance .

1572
01:33:03,078 --> 01:33:06,790
In fact , from what I know of Japanese folk traditions ,

1573
01:33:06,957 --> 01:33:12,129
which I believe inspired the game to a large extent ,

1574
01:33:12,504 --> 01:33:16,133
I think that what 's really happening in this scene

1575
01:33:16,300 --> 01:33:19,219
is that Alessa is visited by her double .

1576
01:33:19,386 --> 01:33:23,348
In her pain she 's been split into several Alessas .

1577
01:33:23,515 --> 01:33:25,642
There 's the real Alessa , suffering ,

1578
01:33:25,809 --> 01:33:29,688
there 's the evil Alessa , her dark side , who 's just visited her ,

1579
01:33:29,855 --> 01:33:32,649
and there 's a good Alessa ,

1580
01:33:34,026 --> 01:33:37,446
who 's made safe in the real world in the shape of Sharon ,

1581
01:33:37,613 --> 01:33:40,908
the little girl adopted by Sean Bean and Radha Mitchell .

1582
01:33:41,074 --> 01:33:43,577
I think that 's the explanation

1583
01:33:44,036 --> 01:33:47,206
for how she becomes a multi-faceted character ,

1584
01:33:47,372 --> 01:33:49,917
with different faces and different ages .

1585
01:33:50,083 --> 01:33:54,797
It 's something that 's in the game , which intrigues players :

1586
01:33:54,963 --> 01:33:59,968
why Alessa exists in different guises , at different ages ...

1587
01:34:00,135 --> 01:34:02,221
Both evil guises

1588
01:34:02,387 --> 01:34:05,140
and guises that are ... " human " .

1589
01:34:05,682 --> 01:34:07,392
I think that 's the explanation .

1590
01:34:07,559 --> 01:34:10,270
In Far-Eastern religions ,

1591
01:34:10,437 --> 01:34:12,773
good and evil aren 't exterior to us .

1592
01:34:12,940 --> 01:34:16,068
We contain our own good and our own evil .

1593
01:34:16,235 --> 01:34:19,404
We are our own god and our own devil .

1594
01:34:19,571 --> 01:34:24,201
I think this is the explanation for what 's happening here in the film .

1595
01:34:24,368 --> 01:34:27,829
Alessa splits into several Alessas .

1596
01:34:33,335 --> 01:34:35,587
That was a very Japanese idea .

1597
01:34:35,754 --> 01:34:40,634
The moment Alessa makes her pact with her evil double ,

1598
01:34:40,801 --> 01:34:44,888
it creates a sort of nuclear fission .

1599
01:34:45,347 --> 01:34:48,725
The hospital bed is the heart of the nuclear reactor ,

1600
01:34:48,892 --> 01:34:52,229
and everything around it is sort of contaminated ...

1601
01:34:54,606 --> 01:34:56,191
by the fission .

1602
01:34:57,609 --> 01:34:59,444
It 's an idea which ...

1603
01:34:59,611 --> 01:35:02,948
We know that , because of Hiroshima and Nagasaki ,

1604
01:35:03,115 --> 01:35:06,326
the Japanese have a deep fear of the atom bomb ,

1605
01:35:06,702 --> 01:35:09,371
nuclear radiation and so on .

1606
01:35:09,538 --> 01:35:13,041
And I think that this is part of Silent Hill :

1607
01:35:13,208 --> 01:35:16,378
the apocalyptic landscape and the ash raining down ,

1608
01:35:16,545 --> 01:35:21,967
like the ash that rained on Hiroshima and Nagasaki after they were bombed .

1609
01:35:22,134 --> 01:35:24,219
All this makes sense , I think .

1610
01:35:24,386 --> 01:35:28,849
I mean that this whole universe like a concentration camp ,

1611
01:35:29,016 --> 01:35:33,020
with its sirens sounding the alarm , with its barbed wire ,

1612
01:35:33,186 --> 01:35:36,523
recalls not only World War II 's concentration camps ,

1613
01:35:36,690 --> 01:35:39,901
but also the nuclear bombs dropped by America

1614
01:35:40,068 --> 01:35:42,529
on Japan at the end of the war .

1615
01:35:42,696 --> 01:35:45,282
I think it all intersects .

1616
01:35:45,949 --> 01:35:47,701
As I 've already said ,

1617
01:35:47,868 --> 01:35:51,288
one ambition of modern art has always been

1618
01:35:51,455 --> 01:35:54,333
to transcribe the century 's great fears ,

1619
01:35:54,499 --> 01:35:56,501
unlike sacred art ,

1620
01:35:56,668 --> 01:35:59,212
which transcribed beauty

1621
01:35:59,379 --> 01:36:02,299
and the elevation of spirit this procured .

1622
01:36:02,466 --> 01:36:04,134
Here , I think that ...

1623
01:36:05,636 --> 01:36:07,721
within this idea

1624
01:36:07,888 --> 01:36:09,931
I 've tried to incorporate the idea

1625
01:36:10,098 --> 01:36:12,726
that Alessa 's hospital bed

1626
01:36:12,893 --> 01:36:17,147
is the heart of the nuclear reactor of all that 's happened .

1627
01:36:22,903 --> 01:36:26,865
This scene shows how mature Jodelle Ferland is .

1628
01:36:27,032 --> 01:36:29,868
Her lines were very complicated ,

1629
01:36:30,035 --> 01:36:33,080
full of biblical references , very symbolic and so on .

1630
01:36:33,246 --> 01:36:36,500
And the way this little girl took charge of her lines

1631
01:36:37,084 --> 01:36:39,211
and made something of them

1632
01:36:39,378 --> 01:36:41,922
was , in my opinion , very special .

1633
01:36:42,255 --> 01:36:45,092
I think Jodelle Ferland is a gifted child .

1634
01:36:45,258 --> 01:36:49,513
I think that a part of the strangeness she radiates

1635
01:36:49,680 --> 01:36:53,183
comes from the fact that she 's gifted , in a real sense .

1636
01:36:53,350 --> 01:36:56,978
I mean that she 's someone who , in a child 's body ,

1637
01:36:57,646 --> 01:37:00,440
has a superior mind .

1638
01:37:00,607 --> 01:37:03,568
I think this made her imposing .

1639
01:37:03,735 --> 01:37:08,824
At the same time , she tried to be the little girl we wanted to see .

1640
01:37:08,991 --> 01:37:13,412
But deep down she 's a much sharper person ,

1641
01:37:13,578 --> 01:37:15,747
much more precise .

1642
01:37:15,914 --> 01:37:20,585
It was always interesting to catch the real Jodelle Ferland by surprise ,

1643
01:37:21,211 --> 01:37:24,339
just as you could catch the real Alessa by surprise .

1644
01:37:24,506 --> 01:37:27,342
There were moments when she 'd play the little girl ,

1645
01:37:27,509 --> 01:37:31,763
and moments when , from the reflective look on her face ,

1646
01:37:31,930 --> 01:37:36,435
you felt she understood a lot more than she was letting on to .

1647
01:37:36,893 --> 01:37:41,440
She taught a thing or two to everyone , even to Radha Mitchell .

1648
01:37:41,606 --> 01:37:44,776
Radha wanted to change one of her lines ,

1649
01:37:44,943 --> 01:37:46,403
and Jodelle said ,

1650
01:37:46,570 --> 01:37:48,655
" No , if you change that line ,

1651
01:37:48,822 --> 01:37:51,700
" my biblical reference won 't make sense . "

1652
01:37:51,867 --> 01:37:55,704
It was quite an amazing thing to witness .

1653
01:37:56,038 --> 01:37:59,624
All it did was increase my admiration for the kid .

1654
01:38:03,962 --> 01:38:07,966
I 'd wanted to work with Deborah Kara Unger for a long time .

1655
01:38:08,133 --> 01:38:11,845
She 'd been the voice of Emu O 'Hara in Crying Freeman .

1656
01:38:12,012 --> 01:38:15,724
She had dubbed the young actress who was in that role ,

1657
01:38:15,891 --> 01:38:19,311
to give her a deeper , more sensual voice .

1658
01:38:19,478 --> 01:38:21,354
So I wanted to film her ,

1659
01:38:21,521 --> 01:38:24,191
and when I showed her the project I said ,

1660
01:38:24,357 --> 01:38:26,318
" Please don 't slap me ! "

1661
01:38:26,485 --> 01:38:30,572
She 's a very beautiful , very sensual woman , you see ,

1662
01:38:30,739 --> 01:38:35,243
and there I was with the project for a film ,

1663
01:38:35,410 --> 01:38:39,873
in which she had to play a disgusting old wreck .

1664
01:38:40,415 --> 01:38:43,794
So I said , " Please don 't slap me !

1665
01:38:43,960 --> 01:38:46,087
" You 've waited all this time ,

1666
01:38:46,254 --> 01:38:49,216
" and now I 'm offering you this grotty character . "

1667
01:38:49,382 --> 01:38:52,928
But she 's an actress , of course , so she was very interested

1668
01:38:53,095 --> 01:38:56,515
in playing such a radically unglamorous character .

1669
01:39:17,160 --> 01:39:21,289
Originally , in the game , you ended up killing Cybil yourself .

1670
01:39:21,456 --> 01:39:24,334
This was quite a traumatic sequence

1671
01:39:24,501 --> 01:39:28,421
because Cybil was the only person who 'd come and helped you .

1672
01:39:28,588 --> 01:39:32,217
But she got possessed and then you had to kill her .

1673
01:39:32,384 --> 01:39:36,138
I decided to give her a different death , a death that 's ...

1674
01:39:36,888 --> 01:39:38,890
much more spectacular .

1675
01:39:39,057 --> 01:39:43,728
She suffers a really awful torment , which is death on the ladder ,

1676
01:39:43,895 --> 01:39:48,358
whereby the person is tied to a ladder and burnt slowly over a fire ,

1677
01:39:48,525 --> 01:39:51,027
and not asphyxiated by the smoke .

1678
01:39:51,194 --> 01:39:55,073
It could take a very long time and is truly horrendous .

1679
01:39:55,240 --> 01:39:58,910
It 's a typical torture of the witch-hunting era .

1680
01:40:00,120 --> 01:40:04,207
When I first started going to see films , including horrors ,

1681
01:40:04,374 --> 01:40:07,502
films about the Inquisition were very popular .

1682
01:40:08,170 --> 01:40:11,715
Films about witches , their trials and executions .

1683
01:40:11,882 --> 01:40:18,763
It was the period just after The Devils , by Ken Russel .

1684
01:40:19,139 --> 01:40:22,183
That had been a big hit but very controversial ,

1685
01:40:22,350 --> 01:40:25,645
and a lot of films were made , especially in Italy ,

1686
01:40:25,812 --> 01:40:28,648
in the wake of that film .

1687
01:40:29,524 --> 01:40:32,736
And for me , horror and fantasy films

1688
01:40:32,903 --> 01:40:36,948
have always been , to some extent , about the combat between ...

1689
01:40:37,282 --> 01:40:40,660
Not good and evil , but the conception of good and evil .

1690
01:40:40,869 --> 01:40:43,663
Because films about witches

1691
01:40:43,830 --> 01:40:47,375
are always subversive and political .

1692
01:40:47,542 --> 01:40:49,586
They usually show

1693
01:40:49,753 --> 01:40:54,799
that witch trials were conducted in very dubious ways ,

1694
01:40:54,966 --> 01:40:57,719
or to keep society from changing ,

1695
01:40:57,886 --> 01:41:00,430
or the status of women from changing .

1696
01:41:01,973 --> 01:41:04,476
And I was very excited , in fact ,

1697
01:41:04,643 --> 01:41:08,897
at the idea of shooting this burning scene in the church ,

1698
01:41:09,064 --> 01:41:11,733
returning to a conception of the fantastic

1699
01:41:11,900 --> 01:41:15,528
which , I have to say , has practically disappeared now ,

1700
01:41:16,571 --> 01:41:18,573
but which I really like

1701
01:41:18,740 --> 01:41:22,619
because , first and foremost , that was my reference point

1702
01:41:22,786 --> 01:41:25,956
when I started going to see horror films .

1703
01:41:30,335 --> 01:41:34,756
One thing that always bothered me about these witch-burning scenes

1704
01:41:34,923 --> 01:41:37,759
was the crap dummy they always burnt .

1705
01:41:37,926 --> 01:41:41,846
It went from , " They 're burning someone , it 's horrible ! "

1706
01:41:42,013 --> 01:41:44,557
to , " Oh , now it 's a dummy ! "

1707
01:41:44,724 --> 01:41:47,560
That was something I couldn 't accept .

1708
01:41:47,727 --> 01:41:48,895
I couldn 't accept

1709
01:41:49,062 --> 01:41:52,816
that they could reduce a person 's martyrdom

1710
01:41:52,983 --> 01:41:56,152
to a grotesque and crude special effect .

1711
01:41:57,404 --> 01:42:01,116
We spent a long time making sure that Cybil 's torment

1712
01:42:01,283 --> 01:42:05,412
in this film was terrifying .

1713
01:42:05,578 --> 01:42:10,083
The moment when you expect me to cut to an old dummy ,

1714
01:42:10,250 --> 01:42:13,378
I show exactly what happens to someone

1715
01:42:13,545 --> 01:42:15,839
tied up above a bonfire .

1716
01:42:20,885 --> 01:42:24,514
I 'll never know how my film got a 12 rating ,

1717
01:42:24,681 --> 01:42:26,933
with scenes like this .

1718
01:42:27,100 --> 01:42:30,562
But that was to the great delight

1719
01:42:30,729 --> 01:42:33,315
of my distributor Samuel Hadida ,

1720
01:42:33,481 --> 01:42:37,569
who didn 't expect us to get such kind treatment

1721
01:42:37,736 --> 01:42:39,571
from the censors .

1722
01:42:39,738 --> 01:42:41,573
But , as I said before ,

1723
01:42:41,740 --> 01:42:45,243
it must have been because it 's in an imaginary world ,

1724
01:42:45,410 --> 01:42:50,165
it 's about a mother 's fight to save her daughter , there are no guns ...

1725
01:42:50,332 --> 01:42:56,338
These are all things which either annoy the censors

1726
01:42:56,504 --> 01:42:57,839
or pacify them .

1727
01:42:58,006 --> 01:43:01,593
But when you get into something

1728
01:43:01,760 --> 01:43:05,597
that 's overtly horrific and straightforwardly atrocious ,

1729
01:43:06,347 --> 01:43:09,059
you have problems . But here ...

1730
01:43:09,392 --> 01:43:14,189
We really didn 't expect to get off that lightly .

1731
01:43:21,321 --> 01:43:23,031
I 'm very proud of this scene

1732
01:43:23,198 --> 01:43:27,368
in terms of organising the space , how the people move and so on .

1733
01:43:27,535 --> 01:43:30,413
Also , as I said a little earlier ,

1734
01:43:30,580 --> 01:43:33,041
I had to do this whole section ,

1735
01:43:33,208 --> 01:43:35,919
from the burning scene onwards ...

1736
01:43:36,628 --> 01:43:39,297
Everything was done in a day and a half ,

1737
01:43:39,923 --> 01:43:42,217
including the final scene ,

1738
01:43:42,383 --> 01:43:46,346
the big final scene in the church , where everyone is killed by Alessa .

1739
01:43:46,513 --> 01:43:48,807
It was an amazing feat ,

1740
01:43:48,973 --> 01:43:53,228
purely in terms of organisation and logistics ,

1741
01:43:53,394 --> 01:43:57,357
managing to do all that in so little time .

1742
01:43:58,650 --> 01:44:00,902
But what I was also trying to do ,

1743
01:44:01,069 --> 01:44:05,657
in order to honour my contract and fit everything in ,

1744
01:44:05,824 --> 01:44:07,909
was shoot it really wide

1745
01:44:08,076 --> 01:44:10,453
- being in Cinemascope helped -

1746
01:44:10,620 --> 01:44:12,122
and really stand back .

1747
01:44:12,288 --> 01:44:14,457
It reminds me of the way

1748
01:44:14,624 --> 01:44:19,504
Roger Corman worked on his series of films inspired by Edgar Allen Poe ,

1749
01:44:20,380 --> 01:44:24,717
which he completed very quickly , sometimes in only three weeks .

1750
01:44:24,884 --> 01:44:27,178
What he did was film wide .

1751
01:44:27,345 --> 01:44:30,557
When people shoot quickly nowadays , they do it close-up .

1752
01:44:32,767 --> 01:44:36,521
But it 's interesting to do it quickly while shooting wide ,

1753
01:44:36,688 --> 01:44:39,357
as long as you have the set and the extras .

1754
01:44:39,524 --> 01:44:43,069
But it is another way of doing it economically .

1755
01:44:49,409 --> 01:44:53,746
When I first read the screenplay , the dialogues , by Roger Avary ,

1756
01:44:54,330 --> 01:44:57,125
I was pretty intrigued

1757
01:44:57,292 --> 01:45:00,044
by the fact that he 'd decided

1758
01:45:00,211 --> 01:45:05,758
to work the final duel between the goody and the baddy

1759
01:45:06,426 --> 01:45:09,262
through dialogue only .

1760
01:45:09,429 --> 01:45:13,600
It had to be a purely dialectical confrontation between Radha Mitchell

1761
01:45:13,933 --> 01:45:15,059
and Alice Krige .

1762
01:45:15,226 --> 01:45:17,896
I thought that was quite daring ,

1763
01:45:18,062 --> 01:45:22,483
because it 's not something you see very often in modern American cinema .

1764
01:45:23,526 --> 01:45:26,404
Duels are expressed

1765
01:45:26,696 --> 01:45:28,448
through physical action ,

1766
01:45:28,615 --> 01:45:31,034
and this was all dialectical .

1767
01:45:31,201 --> 01:45:35,038
And as I didn 't want to make an action movie , I was excited

1768
01:45:35,205 --> 01:45:37,957
at the thought of directing this scene .

1769
01:45:41,794 --> 01:45:43,421
Obviously , this scene ,

1770
01:45:43,588 --> 01:45:45,757
with Radha Mitchell saying ,

1771
01:45:45,924 --> 01:45:49,511
" Your faith brings death , "

1772
01:45:50,762 --> 01:45:54,224
was a scene which caused a few problems

1773
01:45:54,390 --> 01:45:56,601
with certain Americans ,

1774
01:45:56,768 --> 01:45:59,103
as I read on certain forums .

1775
01:45:59,270 --> 01:46:03,942
They probably felt that it was aimed at them ... As it was !

1776
01:46:05,193 --> 01:46:10,615
But besides certain fundamentalist groups in the States ,

1777
01:46:10,782 --> 01:46:14,786
it 's not so much Americans I 'm getting at , as all those people

1778
01:46:15,370 --> 01:46:18,748
who practise an intolerant religion

1779
01:46:18,915 --> 01:46:23,044
and who bring nothing but war , conflict and death .

1780
01:46:23,586 --> 01:46:25,630
So it 's not ...

1781
01:46:25,880 --> 01:46:28,466
Despite the film 's context ,

1782
01:46:28,633 --> 01:46:32,971
I think this criticism applies to all intolerant religions

1783
01:46:33,137 --> 01:46:35,014
and not just

1784
01:46:35,181 --> 01:46:37,350
to sects in the United States .

1785
01:46:40,603 --> 01:46:43,273
Originally , in the script ,

1786
01:46:43,564 --> 01:46:47,986
the door to hell that has been opened

1787
01:46:48,319 --> 01:46:51,906
by Radha Mitchell 's impure blood

1788
01:46:52,949 --> 01:46:57,412
was not going to be the portal for Alessa 's re-ascent ,

1789
01:46:57,579 --> 01:46:59,122
as we 'll see here ,

1790
01:46:59,289 --> 01:47:02,333
but was going to allow the ascent

1791
01:47:02,500 --> 01:47:07,088
of six judges from hell , six Red Pyramids ,

1792
01:47:07,255 --> 01:47:09,132
bearing various weapons ,

1793
01:47:09,299 --> 01:47:12,176
not just the huge sword , but also a spear ,

1794
01:47:12,343 --> 01:47:14,262
a mace and so on .

1795
01:47:14,429 --> 01:47:17,181
And the six judges from hell

1796
01:47:17,348 --> 01:47:19,100
would punish the ...

1797
01:47:21,269 --> 01:47:25,773
the members of the sect , in a sort of pastiche

1798
01:47:26,357 --> 01:47:29,861
of the paintings of Hieronymus Bosch ,

1799
01:47:30,528 --> 01:47:33,406
impaling them one after the other ,

1800
01:47:33,573 --> 01:47:35,825
cutting them in half lengthways and so on .

1801
01:47:35,992 --> 01:47:37,535
A sort of orgiastic scene ,

1802
01:47:37,702 --> 01:47:42,582
filmed like a mediaeval painting of hell .

1803
01:47:42,749 --> 01:47:44,250
Of course ,

1804
01:47:45,209 --> 01:47:47,920
I 'd planned to do it in several passes

1805
01:47:48,087 --> 01:47:51,966
because we only had one Red Pyramid , and not six .

1806
01:47:52,133 --> 01:47:55,428
So I 'd have had to shoot it a bit at a time ,

1807
01:47:56,512 --> 01:47:58,681
and sandwich it together after .

1808
01:47:58,848 --> 01:48:03,186
Obviously , when I heard I only had a day and a half for the ending ,

1809
01:48:03,686 --> 01:48:06,647
including the verbal combat we just saw ,

1810
01:48:06,814 --> 01:48:10,652
I realised that there was no way I could do that .

1811
01:48:10,818 --> 01:48:15,740
So , at the last moment , I had to rethink the final scene ,

1812
01:48:15,907 --> 01:48:18,534
with my storyboarder , Thierry Ségur ,

1813
01:48:18,701 --> 01:48:20,453
a very loyal colleague .

1814
01:48:20,620 --> 01:48:23,456
So we thought up a scene

1815
01:48:23,623 --> 01:48:26,459
which was driven by Japanese references

1816
01:48:26,626 --> 01:48:29,170
and inspired by Japanese animation ,

1817
01:48:29,337 --> 01:48:32,382
especially Urotsukidoji , Legend of the Overfiend ,

1818
01:48:32,548 --> 01:48:34,967
in which the forces of evil

1819
01:48:35,134 --> 01:48:39,055
take on a sort of octopus-like aspect .

1820
01:48:39,222 --> 01:48:42,975
Creatures with tentacles .

1821
01:48:43,643 --> 01:48:48,106
And it was with this reference , in a way , that we finished off the film .

1822
01:48:48,731 --> 01:48:50,942
It 's a sequence I 'm very proud of ,

1823
01:48:51,109 --> 01:48:52,443
not because of the result ,

1824
01:48:52,610 --> 01:48:55,029
but because I did it in a day and a half .

1825
01:48:55,196 --> 01:48:57,031
To tell the truth ,

1826
01:48:57,198 --> 01:48:59,534
I didn 't think I 'd finish the scene .

1827
01:48:59,701 --> 01:49:03,788
I could feel those insurance people watching me ,

1828
01:49:03,955 --> 01:49:08,000
the people from the completion bond .

1829
01:49:08,167 --> 01:49:10,628
They were there on the set ,

1830
01:49:10,795 --> 01:49:13,715
waiting to see how I could finish off the film

1831
01:49:15,508 --> 01:49:17,260
in less than a day .

1832
01:49:17,760 --> 01:49:21,681
I 'm subtracting the time it took to shoot the speeches .

1833
01:49:21,848 --> 01:49:25,309
So it was complicated , but we did it .

1834
01:49:26,269 --> 01:49:28,688
Anyway , it just goes to show

1835
01:49:28,855 --> 01:49:31,774
that when you have a gun pointed at your head

1836
01:49:31,941 --> 01:49:34,610
you can do a lot .

1837
01:49:41,200 --> 01:49:46,747
The idea of the bed with Alessa 's burnt body on it ,

1838
01:49:47,123 --> 01:49:50,293
attacking the members of the sect ,

1839
01:49:52,128 --> 01:49:56,674
does have a basis in the universe of Silent Hill

1840
01:49:56,841 --> 01:50:00,386
because the " boss " at the end of the second game

1841
01:50:00,553 --> 01:50:03,806
is a person in a flying bed ,

1842
01:50:03,973 --> 01:50:05,099
in Silent Hill 2 .

1843
01:50:05,266 --> 01:50:07,769
Bosses are harder to kill

1844
01:50:07,935 --> 01:50:10,605
than the other creatures you meet in a game .

1845
01:50:10,771 --> 01:50:13,357
And this boss really intrigued me ,

1846
01:50:13,524 --> 01:50:16,736
this character strapped into a hospital bed ,

1847
01:50:16,903 --> 01:50:20,740
flying above and attacking the main character .

1848
01:50:21,782 --> 01:50:23,326
It gave me the idea

1849
01:50:23,784 --> 01:50:27,872
that , rather than the six judges , it might be better if Alessa

1850
01:50:28,039 --> 01:50:30,124
delivers out her own vengeance .

1851
01:50:35,505 --> 01:50:39,091
To complete this scene in so little time , then ,

1852
01:50:39,258 --> 01:50:41,219
I favoured wide shots .

1853
01:50:41,385 --> 01:50:43,179
I remembered

1854
01:50:43,346 --> 01:50:47,183
not just Japanese animation , but a famous Disney film ,

1855
01:50:47,350 --> 01:50:48,684
Sleeping Beauty .

1856
01:50:48,851 --> 01:50:51,020
The idea was to try to film

1857
01:50:51,187 --> 01:50:54,398
the creature that has burst out of hell

1858
01:50:54,565 --> 01:50:58,402
like the evil thorn bush which guards Sleeping Beauty 's castle .

1859
01:50:59,153 --> 01:51:01,280
So I favoured wide shots ,

1860
01:51:01,447 --> 01:51:04,951
and favoured the movement of the thorn bush 's branches ,

1861
01:51:05,117 --> 01:51:08,830
or , in this case , the tentacles of barbed wire ,

1862
01:51:08,996 --> 01:51:10,498
within the decor .

1863
01:51:10,665 --> 01:51:14,168
The dynamism isn 't in the directing , but in the movement

1864
01:51:14,669 --> 01:51:18,506
of what would be added later : the barbed-wire tentacles .

1865
01:51:22,677 --> 01:51:26,264
The special effects were done , once again , by BUF .

1866
01:51:26,430 --> 01:51:28,391
They didn 't do all the film 's VFX .

1867
01:51:28,558 --> 01:51:31,936
Lots were done by Canadian companies and so on .

1868
01:51:32,103 --> 01:51:35,439
But they did the really big chunks of the film

1869
01:51:36,607 --> 01:51:39,360
where the focus was on the stylised aspect .

1870
01:51:44,949 --> 01:51:47,994
One moment that it was important to get right

1871
01:51:48,160 --> 01:51:51,122
was when Alessa confronts Sharon ,

1872
01:51:51,289 --> 01:51:54,834
when the dark Alessa , her evil side ,

1873
01:51:55,209 --> 01:51:58,796
meets her good side , Sharon ,

1874
01:51:58,963 --> 01:52:01,924
and the two fuse together again .

1875
01:52:02,091 --> 01:52:05,636
I thought it 'd be very difficult to find a young actress

1876
01:52:05,803 --> 01:52:09,890
who could pull off that feat , and play both at the same time .

1877
01:52:10,057 --> 01:52:13,603
And I think Jodelle did a great job .

1878
01:52:16,522 --> 01:52:19,275
The conclusion of the film , of course ,

1879
01:52:19,442 --> 01:52:23,654
was rather tricky to imagine .

1880
01:52:25,323 --> 01:52:27,116
After all the madness ,

1881
01:52:27,450 --> 01:52:31,370
that long journey through a dreamlike world ,

1882
01:52:31,537 --> 01:52:33,664
how could I finish the film ?

1883
01:52:34,415 --> 01:52:37,251
Going on the principle of The Twilight Zone ,

1884
01:52:37,418 --> 01:52:41,964
what final twist could I give to the film ?

1885
01:52:42,214 --> 01:52:47,678
So I went for the Judaeo-Christian explanation ,

1886
01:52:48,304 --> 01:52:52,391
the idea that the character is in purgatory ,

1887
01:52:52,558 --> 01:52:55,811
and can 't escape from that dimension .

1888
01:52:55,978 --> 01:52:58,022
She escaped from hell ,

1889
01:52:58,189 --> 01:53:02,568
but she can 't escape from the fact that maybe she 's dead

1890
01:53:02,735 --> 01:53:06,405
or , in any case , no longer belongs to our world ,

1891
01:53:06,572 --> 01:53:10,493
and remains in limbo between two worlds ,

1892
01:53:10,660 --> 01:53:13,621
trapped between two dimensions .

1893
01:53:13,788 --> 01:53:18,834
It was through thinking about certain episodes of The Twilight Zone ,

1894
01:53:19,001 --> 01:53:23,547
particularly one called Little Girl Lost , which inspired Poltergeist ,

1895
01:53:23,714 --> 01:53:28,594
an episode where a girl slipped through a crack in one dimension

1896
01:53:28,761 --> 01:53:33,849
and found herself stuck between our world and another ...

1897
01:53:34,016 --> 01:53:36,560
It was thinking about that that I imagined

1898
01:53:36,727 --> 01:53:38,688
the end of Silent Hill .

1899
01:53:39,271 --> 01:53:42,191
At the same time , I wanted a happy ending .

1900
01:53:42,358 --> 01:53:45,986
After all , she has found her lost daughter ,

1901
01:53:46,153 --> 01:53:49,240
she brings her back with her and so on .

1902
01:53:49,532 --> 01:53:53,703
Except that what she brings back is different from what she lost .

1903
01:53:53,869 --> 01:53:57,415
But I tried to respect that happy ending ,

1904
01:53:57,581 --> 01:54:00,459
the calm after the storm ,

1905
01:54:00,626 --> 01:54:04,922
the calm after the massacre of all the sect members .

1906
01:54:06,674 --> 01:54:10,386
In this ending there 's an idea I 've always liked in the fantastic ,

1907
01:54:10,553 --> 01:54:16,016
which is that ghosts are just fragments of our memories ,

1908
01:54:16,183 --> 01:54:20,062
that we create ghosts ourselves , with our memories ,

1909
01:54:20,229 --> 01:54:22,106
with our expectations and so on .

1910
01:54:22,273 --> 01:54:26,193
It 's an idea I came across in Amenábar 's The Others ,

1911
01:54:26,360 --> 01:54:27,778
a magnificent film ,

1912
01:54:27,945 --> 01:54:32,158
where the people you thought were the main characters

1913
01:54:32,324 --> 01:54:34,827
are the ghosts doing the haunting ,

1914
01:54:34,994 --> 01:54:39,331
although you 'd assumed by reflex that it was the other way round .

1915
01:54:39,582 --> 01:54:43,961
And here , in fact , Radha Mitchell 's character

1916
01:54:44,128 --> 01:54:47,923
will become , in a way , a ghost to her husband .

1917
01:54:48,090 --> 01:54:52,511
He 'll go on waiting for her but she 'll never walk in the door .

1918
01:54:52,678 --> 01:54:54,972
I thought it was very interesting

1919
01:54:55,139 --> 01:54:58,893
that you leave the film understanding that the main character

1920
01:54:59,059 --> 01:55:01,729
has become one of its creatures or ghosts ,

1921
01:55:01,896 --> 01:55:05,024
one of its most melancholic creatures .

1922
01:55:07,526 --> 01:55:09,987
The film came out

1923
01:55:10,154 --> 01:55:14,408
during a wave of horror films in the States ,

1924
01:55:14,575 --> 01:55:19,580
launched by Saw and other successes like Hostel .

1925
01:55:21,165 --> 01:55:23,542
And it 's probably because of that

1926
01:55:23,709 --> 01:55:25,503
that the people at TriStar

1927
01:55:25,669 --> 01:55:28,005
needed to get the film out ,

1928
01:55:28,172 --> 01:55:30,174
were nice to me and so on .

1929
01:55:30,341 --> 01:55:34,386
There was this idea that a European auteur or director

1930
01:55:34,845 --> 01:55:38,724
could bring a special sort of approach to a film ,

1931
01:55:38,891 --> 01:55:41,644
which was going to do well anyway .

1932
01:55:41,811 --> 01:55:44,897
They were absolutely certain it was going to do well .

1933
01:55:45,064 --> 01:55:48,108
I remember that my editor Sébastien Prangère and I

1934
01:55:48,275 --> 01:55:50,736
were really puzzled

1935
01:55:51,403 --> 01:55:55,199
by how certain the people at TriStar were ,

1936
01:55:55,366 --> 01:55:57,368
even as post-production begun ,

1937
01:55:57,535 --> 01:55:59,078
that it would do well .

1938
01:55:59,245 --> 01:56:01,455
And they weren 't wrong .

1939
01:56:01,622 --> 01:56:04,917
The film did exactly as they predicted ,

1940
01:56:05,084 --> 01:56:10,422
exactly as they predicted it would on the weekend of its release .

1941
01:56:10,589 --> 01:56:13,259
Which means that these people

1942
01:56:13,425 --> 01:56:16,846
had perfectly analysed the situation ,

1943
01:56:17,012 --> 01:56:20,015
which was that horror films were " in " again .

1944
01:56:20,182 --> 01:56:24,895
So the film came out during that wave and was a hit .

1945
01:56:25,062 --> 01:56:28,941
That wave is in decline at the moment , it 's dying down .

1946
01:56:29,108 --> 01:56:30,901
People have had their fill .

1947
01:56:31,068 --> 01:56:35,781
During that wave , one horror film a week was being released in the States .

1948
01:56:35,948 --> 01:56:37,116
That 's incredible .

1949
01:56:37,283 --> 01:56:41,120
And almost all of them did well at the box office .

1950
01:56:42,121 --> 01:56:44,957
When I was working on Silent Hill ,

1951
01:56:45,124 --> 01:56:50,087
it gave me a feeling of security to be working in that climate ,

1952
01:56:50,254 --> 01:56:54,049
where we were making horror films because people wanted them .

1953
01:56:54,216 --> 01:56:56,552
But I was also a bit obsessed

1954
01:56:56,719 --> 01:57:01,182
with the idea of making a horror film like no other ,

1955
01:57:01,348 --> 01:57:04,268
with a very distinct atmosphere and so on .

1956
01:57:04,435 --> 01:57:06,854
I didn 't want people to forget this film .

1957
01:57:07,021 --> 01:57:10,441
Love it or loathe it , but not forget it .

1958
01:57:10,608 --> 01:57:15,154
I 'd be annoyed if they forgot what a weird film it was .

1959
01:57:16,197 --> 01:57:21,201
I think that my efforts were aided , quite distinctly , by the idea

1960
01:57:21,368 --> 01:57:24,622
that , thanks to the material of the video game ,

1961
01:57:24,788 --> 01:57:29,168
this film would always remain in a class of its own ,

1962
01:57:29,335 --> 01:57:32,504
whatever its artistic merits might be .

1963
01:57:35,674 --> 01:57:39,219
A lot of people have wondered why I ended the film

1964
01:57:39,386 --> 01:57:43,599
with the rain falling on this bush .

1965
01:57:43,766 --> 01:57:46,810
In fact , that too is ...

1966
01:57:46,977 --> 01:57:48,771
a very Japanese shot ,

1967
01:57:48,938 --> 01:57:53,317
to show the character 's inner atmosphere , his melancholy .

1968
01:57:53,484 --> 01:57:58,072
I 'm doing what in Japanese poetry they call a haiku .

1969
01:57:58,238 --> 01:58:02,743
I 'm showing his inner state

1970
01:58:02,910 --> 01:58:08,082
simply by watching the rain falling on the vegetation .

1971
01:58:08,374 --> 01:58:11,752
That 's why I ended the film like that .

1972
01:58:11,919 --> 01:58:14,630
It was a way of defining

1973
01:58:14,797 --> 01:58:17,174
what the character 's life would now be :

1974
01:58:17,341 --> 01:58:19,969
an interminable waiting ,

1975
01:58:20,135 --> 01:58:24,515
thinking his wife is close by , which is sort of the case ,

1976
01:58:24,682 --> 01:58:27,226
but never seeing her again .

1977
01:58:28,227 --> 01:58:31,939
The end credits of the film are a direct homage

1978
01:58:32,106 --> 01:58:33,899
to the game

1979
01:58:34,066 --> 01:58:36,568
and its own particular aesthetic .

1980
01:58:36,735 --> 01:58:39,780
They were entirely designed by Kook Ewo ,

1981
01:58:40,698 --> 01:58:42,658
who did the flashback .

1982
01:58:42,825 --> 01:58:46,412
I gave the job to him . It wasn 't planned originally .

1983
01:58:46,578 --> 01:58:51,083
And he literally put it all together by himself ,

1984
01:58:51,250 --> 01:58:54,295
all on his own , sitting in a room ,

1985
01:58:54,461 --> 01:58:56,588
creating these amazing credits ,

1986
01:58:56,755 --> 01:59:01,260
which are , I think , a lovely homage to the Konami game .

1987
01:59:04,388 --> 01:59:07,224
I thought that was a nice way to end the film ,

1988
01:59:07,391 --> 01:59:09,268
reminding you of its roots ,

1989
01:59:09,435 --> 01:59:13,105
reminding you that the film is an adaptation

1990
01:59:13,897 --> 01:59:17,568
of what , in my opinion , is a genuine work of art ,

1991
01:59:17,735 --> 01:59:20,362
maybe a minor work of art ,

1992
01:59:20,529 --> 01:59:22,364
but art all the same .

1993
01:59:22,531 --> 01:59:24,325
I think that , in a few years ,

1994
01:59:24,491 --> 01:59:29,663
when the first historians of video games consider these games ,

1995
01:59:30,497 --> 01:59:34,251
they 'll grant Silent Hill the place it deserves ,

1996
01:59:34,418 --> 01:59:37,629
the place of a game that is absolutely mind-blowing ,

1997
01:59:38,547 --> 01:59:40,591
which has no equivalent ,

1998
01:59:40,758 --> 01:59:45,512
which is an original , unique work , one of a kind ,

1999
01:59:45,679 --> 01:59:49,892
which I was delighted to be able to adapt for the cinema .

2000
01:59:50,059 --> 01:59:52,394
I did it with passion .

2001
01:59:53,103 --> 01:59:55,689
Before being just another film ,

2002
01:59:55,856 --> 01:59:58,192
I think that Silent Hill

2003
01:59:58,359 --> 02:00:02,071
is a reflection of my passion for video games in general ,

2004
02:00:02,237 --> 02:00:07,034
for this oeuvre , Silent Hill , and for the people who created it .

2005
02:00:09,661 --> 02:00:13,415
It was my way of doffing my hat

2006
02:00:13,624 --> 02:00:15,250
to a parallel art ,

2007
02:00:15,417 --> 02:00:16,877
which , I hope ,

2008
02:00:17,044 --> 02:00:20,964
will be very soon be appreciated at its true value .

2009
02:00:22,132 --> 02:00:26,345
Well , thank you for listening to me during this film .

2010
02:00:28,013 --> 02:00:31,225
Keep going to see films and keep playing video games .

2011
02:00:31,391 --> 02:00:32,351
Bye .

2012
02:05:11,880 --> 02:05:14,508
Subtitles : Howard Bonsor

2013
02:05:14,675 --> 02:05:17,135
Subtitling : C.M.C. - Paris

